('lessons', '1790397651557218', $irr${"blocks": [{"caption": "", "src": "/uploads/20b71bd8e8b516df367142ad8a53ac6c.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "To give great hospitality to your customers, you have to care about their experience.<br>1. Ask yourself -  will they enjoy themselves enough to come back?<br><br>2. Find out what they want - Do they know about the product? Do they know what they want, or do they need your help finding it?<br><br>3. Show them that you care - If your goal is to create a great experience for customers, start by showing them warmth and enthusiasm.<br><br>4. Get them what they want or the next best thing - To do this well, you need to be accurate and efficient.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "These days, good service are hard to find, and the differences in quality between one and the next can be quite subtle. If you lose the trust of a customer one day, they will most likely not come back. Great-tasting food and beverage is a wonderful and precious thing, but community-building and positive human interactions matter more than coffee flavour. ", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "Defining Great Hospitality", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790406414785721', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Eye contact - Anyone nearby (outside, at the door, or at the bar) should feel noticed. You don&#39;t need to smile, but you do need to show you&#39;re ready to serve. That first glance is where the welcome starts.<br><br>Greeting - If a guest is close enough to hear you, say something, even before you take their order. When there&#39;s a queue, you owe something to everyone waiting, not just the person in front of you. At busy times, keep the greeting brief. Guests will forgive a short greeting if they can see confident, organised energy.<br><br>Do you recognise the customer? - If you don&#39;t, treat them as new. This one interaction can decide whether they become a regular who spends a lot over the year, or a guest who never comes back.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "", "src": "/uploads/a763d662d1df581e8e6b3751ec470a61.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "Regulars - Remembering every name is hard. Till systems, or asking for a name when you take the order, can help jog your memory. Other ways to show you remember them: Remembering their usual drink. A look that shows you recognise them.<br><br><b>Tailoring the experience</b><br><br>Good hospitality is fitted to each person, and some guests don&#39;t want to chat. For example, a well-known person may want to go unnoticed.<br>They might not be awake enough to engage with you. <br>They might have a hangover.<br>They just like to be left alone.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 1, "title": "Face to Face", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790406838985408', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Listen carefully", "t": "p"}, {"html": "First, find out exactly what the guest wants. Listen to every word, and taking notes is fine.<br>Watch for key details:<br>Allergies<br>Drink strength<br>Temperature (someone carrying a drink on public transport may not want it too hot)", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Your turn to respond", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Be realistic. Don&#39;t raise false hopes, but do suggest something if you think it will suit them.<br>Use your intuition. That might mean a small surprise, like a taste of something new. Or it might mean the best thing you can do is make their order quickly.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Keep the conversation simple", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Some guests love coffee knowledge; for others, especially people in a hurry, it&#39;s a nuisance. Wait for the guest to show interest.<br>Don&#39;t make things up. The author once guessed wrong about decaffeination, and a guest from Switzerland corrected him. (Swiss Water decaffeination was invented in Switzerland in the 1930s, but the company that runs it is based in Canada.) The lesson: admit what you don&#39;t know, because some guests know more than you do.<br>Share your knowledge over several visits rather than all at once. An inspired guest will come back.<br>Don&#39;t let one great conversation make you neglect the queue.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Don&#39;t pry<br><br>Wait for guests to invite a personal conversation.<br>Guests want different things: some want to chat and learn, some want to work, and some just want to enjoy their coffee in peace.<br>If someone looks upset, it&#39;s kind to ask if they&#39;re okay, but don&#39;t question them. Respecting privacy is part of good hospitality.<br><br>Case study: parenting<br><br>The advice is not to try to make a happy baby happier. A guest absorbed in a book with their coffee is best left alone until they need something.<br>Read each guest&#39;s face and body language to judge what kind of interaction they&#39;d welcome.<br><br>Case study: Columbia Road Flower Market (London)<br><br>A famous actor visited a coffee cart every week. Some weeks she came in disguise and wanted to be left alone; other weeks she was happy to chat.<br>Her appearance told the baristas how to approach her. Sometimes you show you admire someone without saying anything.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 2, "title": "Interacting with Customers", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790407464068074', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Accept Praise - <br>Take compliments gracefully. If a guest praises your work, just say thank you. Don&#39;t downplay it or argue, even if you think it wasn&#39;t your best work, like a latte art pour you&#39;re not happy with.<br>Don&#39;t fish for praise or tips. When you&#39;ve done something exceptional, never make the guest feel they owe you a compliment or a tip.<br><br>Respect the guest&#39;s choice about money - <br>How much a guest spends or tips is their decision. Assume the best, since they may have no change or be on a tight budget.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Be Gracious About Complaints", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Mistakes happen everywhere. Even the best venues get service wrong sometimes, so expect complaints and plan for them.<br><br>A complaint made to your face is a gift. It&#39;s much better than a bad online review you can&#39;t control or answer properly. Thank the guest for telling you.<br>Treat it as useful information. A complaint shows you how guests actually see your hospitality and product quality.<br><br>Make the guest feel heard. Acknowledge what they said (&quot;I hear you&quot;). Excuses rarely help, but an apology almost always does.<br><br>Own the mistake. If it&#39;s your error, say so plainly.<br><br>Offer to remake it. It&#39;s almost always worth remaking a drink or dish. The one exception is when a second remake would come out exactly the same. Then offer something different that better suits what they want. The course example is a guest who finds a pour-over too weak: don&#39;t argue, offer a stronger-tasting drink like a long black.<br><br>The payoff: Handle it well and you may gain a regular, and you&#39;ll know their preferences next time.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "What this means for your restaurants: it lines up with a simple recovery routine for every location: listen, apologise, own it, fix it or offer an alternative, then log it. The log is what lets managers spot patterns, like one dish or one shift causing repeat complaints.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 3, "title": "Compliments and Criticism", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790407696985519', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Why it matters", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Cafés and restaurants are unpredictable, and a tense incident can leave staff shaken for days. Every venue needs a plan in place before one happens.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "1. Unwanted attention from guests<br>Being friendly with regulars is fine, but staff should set personal boundaries as early as possible if a guest wants more attention than they&#39;re comfortable with.<br>Staff don&#39;t have to deal with it alone. Managers or HR should step in and support them.<br>Staff owe no explanation for turning down contact outside work. They just need to stay clear and respectful.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "2. Aggressive guests: why hospitality needs its own approach<br>Banks lock down and nightclubs hire security. Cafés have neither, so staff need a clear protocol for what to do when a guest behaves inappropriately.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "3. Handling an aggressive guest<br>Aggression is never acceptable, but you can&#39;t reason with someone who is enraged.<br>Stay calm, or at least look calm. Anger often comes from distress or fear, so responding with empathy rather than anger helps de-escalate.<br>The goal is safety, not winning. Staff don&#39;t need to stop the guest being angry. They need to keep themselves, colleagues and other guests safe.<br>Step away if threatened. No one is expected to risk physical harm for the job. Staff should step back and let a manager or colleague take over.<br><br>4. Responsibilities for managers and owners<br>Be ready to step in and de-escalate yourself.<br>Talk the contingency plan through with the whole team before an incident happens.<br>Consider professional de-escalation training for some or all of the team.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 4, "title": "Managing Bad Situations", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790407846252212', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "What to do", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Look calm and confident, even if you don&#39;t feel that way.<br>Keep your only goal in mind: helping the person calm down.<br>Speak in a low, slow, even voice.<br>Treat the person with dignity, let insults pass, and don&#39;t judge.<br>Give them plenty of space, about four times your usual distance.<br>Acknowledge their feelings without accepting their behaviour. For example: &quot;I can see you&#39;re upset, but you can&#39;t block the counter.&quot;<br>Ask them to help you understand what&#39;s upset them, rather than asking how they feel.<br>Suggest a simple next step, like moving somewhere quieter.<br>Offer choices so they feel some sense of control.<br>Put your own safety first, and remember you can always walk away.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "What to avoid", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Don&#39;t rush in. Take a moment to read their body language and tone, and plan how you&#39;d get away.<br>Don&#39;t raise your voice or try to shout over them.<br>Don&#39;t ask how they&#39;re feeling or try to interpret their emotions.<br>Don&#39;t respond to abusive questions.<br>Don&#39;t smile, as it can look like mockery.<br>Don&#39;t touch the person. Even friendly contact can feel threatening to someone agitated.<br>Don&#39;t argue or explain. Offer choices instead.<br>Don&#39;t put your hands in your pockets. Keep them visible and ready to protect yourself.<br>Don&#39;t point or wag a finger.<br>Don&#39;t hold constant eye contact. Let them look away.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 20, "pos": 5, "title": "De - Escalation, Do's and Don'ts", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790407976518169', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "The core rule", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Coworkers won&#39;t always get along, but guests should never be pulled into staff disputes.<br>Never complain to a guest about a colleague. Your problems shouldn&#39;t become their problems.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Bar scenario", "t": "p"}, {"html": "A manager trains a new staff member to pour a pint in front of a waiting guest. The manager knows his stuff but comes across as brusque.<br>Once the manager walks off, the trainee rolls his eyes and apologises to the guest for him. That undermines the team and makes the guest uncomfortable.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "How to improve it (the lesson&#39;s quiz)", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Train before the floor. Train new staff when no guests are around, before or after service. It costs extra time and wages, but it&#39;s worth it.<br>Don&#39;t make the guest wait on training. Telling a guest to hold on while training happens in front of them is poor hospitality.<br>Never roll your eyes or show frustration with a colleague in front of guests.<br>Win guests over with warmth and enthusiasm, not by siding with them against a teammate.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Case study: Madeleine Geach&#39;s five steps for better team dynamics<br><br>Take a step back. Reflect on your impact as a leader and what your team needs.<br>Set three non-negotiables. Pick the key standards that matter most for team performance.<br>Communicate constantly. Repeat important messages consistently.<br>Inject positivity. Aim for about five pieces of positive feedback for every criticism. The lesson cites the Gottman Institute for this ratio.<br>End the day well. Close each shift on positives to lift the mood.<br><br>Reflection point<br><br>Think about how you&#39;ve spoken with colleagues this week. If criticism outweighs praise, expect tension in the team.<br><br>What this means for your restaurants:<br><br>Ban on-floor training during peak service at all four sites, and schedule it before opening or during quiet periods instead.<br>Ask each site manager to name their three non-negotiables so standards stay consistent across locations.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 6, "title": "Getting Along with Co-workers", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790408099836107', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "he core idea", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Every coffee business has its own model. Some are big chains, some are small groups of 5–10 sites, and many are single sites.<br>Picture a spectrum running from fast food at one end to fine dining at the other. Station kiosks, espresso and brunch bars, and brew bars sit in between.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "What decides where a business sits on the spectrum", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Price point compared with competitors<br>Location and size of the premises<br>Number of staff<br>How much automated equipment it uses<br>How much it values human interaction over speed", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Two models in one venue", "t": "p"}, {"html": "A fine-dining restaurant can run two models at once:<br>Front of house: a lot of personal interaction, like guiding guests through the menu or explaining wine pairings.<br>Kitchen: built for speed and precision.<br>A small café is different. Staff often cover several roles at once, such as serving guests and making drinks.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Fast food model<br><br>Little or no emphasis on human interaction. Guests order at a machine, a highly automated kitchen makes the food, and the only contact is an order number being called.<br>Rent and equipment costs are high, but it works because of sheer speed and volume.<br><br>Train station coffee kiosk<br><br>The model is built around speed. Sites are small, rents are high and most sales are takeaway.<br>They usually rely heavily on automation, such as bean-to-cup machines.<br>Good hospitality here means fast, accurate service. Most guests don&#39;t want conversation.<br>Typical volume: 400–600 drinks per espresso machine per day.<br><br>Coffee bar and brunch restaurants<br><br>They run like restaurants. Guests are seated and FOH staff take orders at the table.<br>Great hospitality depends on teamwork.<br>Typical volume: 200–400 drinks per espresso machine per day.<br><br>The fine-dining coffee model<br><br>Some coffee shops run like fine-dining restaurants, especially those with brew bars built around filter coffee.<br>They&#39;re rare, but they have a distinctive place in the coffee world.<br><br>How brew bars (slow bars) work<br><br>Each drink takes much longer to make than at an espresso bar, so the guest gets a more immersive experience.<br>The trade-off is much higher staff cost per drink.<br>In return, staff have far more time to talk with guests and offer warm, personal service.<br>Typical volume: 30–40 drinks per day.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790397628655983, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 7, "title": "Service Style", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790750652149267', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "<b>Spotting ripeness:</b> Unripe cherries are shades of green. Overripe ones run from greyish-red or yellow through to black. <br><br>Pickers take only the ripe cherries. This gives higher-scoring coffee but costs more, because it takes much more labour. Pickers clear the whole branch in one pass. Mechanical harvesting is one form of it: it pulls off ripe and overripe cherries alike and leaves only the firmest unripe ones on the tree.<br><br>Regions with a clear dry season, like Minas Gerais and Paraná in Brazil, harvest during that season. They flower only once or twice a year, so pickers go back to each tree fewer times and selective picking costs less. Regions with rain spread through the year flower many times, so trees need many more passes.<br><br>In parts of Colombia, where coffee flowers almost every month, picking is 58% of a farm&#39;s total labour cost. In Costa Rica, with 12–15 flowerings a year, harvesting is 41%.<br><br>Weight burden: The green bean is only 18% of the cherry&#39;s weight before drying. That means pickers carry more than five times the weight of the final product from tree to mill.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "What limits how fast a farm can harvest - how evenly the cherries ripen, yield per hectare, how densely the trees are planted, and how steep the slope is.<br><br>\tSelective harvesting - Output per worker per day\t50–120 kg of cherries<br>Quality\tConsistent ripeness, higher scores, After harvest\tLittle sorting needed<br><br>Strip picking - Output per worker per day 120–250 kg of cherries, Quality Wide spread of ripeness and many &quot;quakers&quot; (defective, underdeveloped beans), After harvest Much more sorting needed to score well (colour sorting machines speed this up).<br><br>Selective picking goes faster when the ripe cherries on each branch are uniform, the branches are heavily loaded, the trees are kept short and the slope is gentle.<br>Strip picking and the natural process go together: Unripe cherries have too little sugar to ferment, so they can&#39;t go through the washed process. They can still be dried and hulled with riper cherries in the natural process, so strip picking has historically been common where natural processing dominates.<br><br>Electric rakes, a middle ground: Borrowed from olive harvesting and now seen in Brazil, these are hand-held poles with vibrating tendrils. Unripe cherries cling more firmly, so pickers can turn the vibration down to leave them on the tree and come back a few weeks later. The cherries fall onto mats on the ground, or into a stiff cone-shaped collar around the tree that funnels them into a bag.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790750510516714, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 0, "title": "The Harvest", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790751069783128', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Mechanical harvesters were developed in the 1980s, first produced in Brazil and the US and more recently in Australia. As Thompson Owen explains in this video (below) the business model of countries with relatively high wage costs such as Australia, Hawaii and Brazil cannot afford the labour costs to employ large groups of hand pickers anymore. Owen feels it is unlikely the job role of coffee picker will continue to be attractive to farm workers as these many coffee producing countries in South and Central America continue to industrialise.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "", "src": "", "t": "video", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZZ5Jm-w8YzU"}, {"html": "Farms that are situated on relatively flat land have recourse to using fully mechanised harvesters such as the ones that you see in the video below. These self-propelled machines can harvest a whole tree in 2–3 seconds (Skybury, 2016). For these harvesters to be viable, trees must be planted in neat rows, with enough space for the harvester to fit between rows. The harvester arches over the whole plant and the foliage is combed by two rotating spindles which pass on either side of the coffee plant’s main trunk. The spindles usually have flexible fiberglass arms so as not to do too much damage to the branches of the tree. The cherry fall onto a set of slats known as a ‘fish plate’ — because it looks like the scales of a fish — at the bottom of the trunk of the tree. The slats catch the cherries after they fall off the branches, guiding them into channels at each side of the harvester. From here, cherries are guided into pneumatic separators to help remove twigs and leaves. Cherries are then blown out into a trailer pulled by a tractor, which is travelling through a neighbouring row (J.N. Wintgens, 2004).", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Where space is more restricted, and the contour of the land is a little steeper but not so steep that a tractor cannot be used, there is a single spindle system designed to trail behind a tractor which allows the driver to adjust the lateral angle of the spindle as well as how high it is vertically. An added advantage of tractor-driven machines compared with self propelled harvesters is that they are a lot cheaper.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "", "src": "/uploads/55ccddd008adb30a94301363bbfdc009.jpg", "t": "img"}], "chapterId": 1790750510516714, "content": "", "minutes": 0, "pos": 1, "title": "Mechanical Harvesters", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790755483833660', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "What you will learn"}, {"items": ["The main ways to assess a trainee, and when to use each.", "Where the post-trial observation fits, and who decides the outcome."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Assessment answers one question: can this person do the job to standard, on their own? Use more than one method, because each shows something different.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Four ways to assess"}, {"items": ["<b>Observation:</b> watch them do the task. Best for skills.", "<b>Questioning:</b> ask recall, reason and scenario questions. Best for knowledge and judgment.", "<b>The finished result:</b> check the plate, the set table, the clean station against the standard.", "<b>Tests:</b> the iTrain course test checks knowledge the same way for everyone."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Match the method to what you are assessing"}, {"items": ["<b>Knowledge</b> (menu, allergens, procedures): questions and tests.", "<b>Skill</b> (carrying, plating, coffee): observation and the finished result.", "<b>Attitude</b> (warmth, teamwork, care): observation over several shifts, and feedback from colleagues and guests."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "The post-trial observation"}, {"html": "At the end of the initial training period, all of these come together in one final check: the trainee is observed working a normal shift. It confirms they can work to standard on their own, in real conditions, before they are fully released onto the rota.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Can they do the core tasks to standard without support?", "Do standards hold up when it is busy?", "Do they show the right attitude with guests and the team?", "Are there gaps that need more training?"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Who decides what"}, {"items": ["<b>The trainer</b> assesses, records the facts and makes a recommendation.", "<b>The manager</b> makes the final decision on the outcome."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<i>Note for the GM:</i> confirm how long the trial period is for each role, and who carries out the observation, before publishing.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Use observation, questions, the finished result and tests.", "Match the method to knowledge, skill or attitude.", "The post-trial observation is the final check in real service.", "The trainer recommends. The manager decides."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Assessment Methods and the Post-Trial Observation", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790755896126304', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Every trainee is different. Some are new to the industry, some are experienced, some are nervous, and some are working in a second language. Knowing who you are training helps you choose the right approach.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Common types of learner"}, {"items": ["<b>The first-timer:</b> new to hospitality. Needs structure, small steps and reassurance.", "<b>The experienced hire:</b> knows the work, may have habits to correct. Needs respect and clear reasons.", "<b>The quick learner:</b> picks things up fast. Needs pace and stretch, but still check every standard.", "<b>The careful learner:</b> slower but thorough. Needs time and repetition, not pressure.", "<b>The quiet or second-language learner:</b> may understand more than they show. Needs visuals, demonstration and safe ways to ask questions."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to find out"}, {"items": ["Ask about their previous experience on day one.", "Ask how they like to learn something new.", "Watch how they respond in the first sessions: do they ask questions, take notes, prefer to try straight away?", "Check understanding early to see where they really are."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A word of caution"}, {"html": "You may have heard of “learning styles”, the idea that people learn only by seeing, hearing or doing. Research does not support matching training to a single style. Everyone learns best with a mix: explain, show, then let them do it. Use what you learn about the person to adjust the pace, support and examples, not to cut out methods.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Adapt the approach to the person. Never adapt the standard.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Find out about the trainee early.", "Adjust pace, support and examples to the person.", "Use a mix of tell, show and do for everyone."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "Identifying the Type Of Learner", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790756031475032', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Knowledge is what a trainee needs to know: menu items, ingredients, allergens, prices, procedures and rules. A knowledge session should be short, focused and interactive. Thirty minutes is enough for one topic.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Before the session"}, {"items": ["Pick <b>one topic</b>, such as the new lunch menu or the allergen procedure.", "Write the objective: “By the end, the trainee will be able to…”", "Prepare the real materials: the menu, the dish, the SOP, photos.", "Choose a quiet time and place, away from service."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A 30-minute structure"}, {"items": ["<b>0–5 min: Why.</b> Explain why this matters to the guest and to them.", "<b>5–15 min: Show and explain.</b> Go through the content in small chunks. Use the real item wherever possible: let them see, smell or taste it.", "<b>15–25 min: Check.</b> Ask questions, use scenarios: “A guest asks if this is dairy-free. What do you say?”", "<b>25–30 min: Recap.</b> Ask them to tell you the three most important points. Agree what happens next."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "If you talk for the whole 30 minutes, they will remember very little. Ask more than you tell.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Making it stick"}, {"items": ["Link new facts to what they already know.", "Use stories and real guest situations.", "Repeat the key points again at the next shift.", "Ask them to explain it to a colleague. Teaching it back is a strong check."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["One topic, one objective, 30 minutes.", "Use the real menu, dish or SOP.", "Check with questions and scenarios, not “Any questions?”"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754638147226, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "Knowledge", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790756047075768', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A skill is something the trainee must be able to do: carry plates, use the till, make a coffee, plate a dish, clean a station. Skills are learned by watching and then doing, many times, with feedback.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to demonstrate well"}, {"items": ["<b>Position.</b> Stand so the trainee sees the task from their own angle, usually beside you, not opposite.", "<b>Full speed first.</b> Show the whole task at normal speed so they see what “good” looks like.", "<b>Slow and step by step.</b> Repeat it slowly, naming each step and the key point.", "<b>Explain why.</b> Give the reason for any step that looks optional.", "<b>Their turn.</b> Let them do it while you watch. Step in only for safety."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A 30-minute structure"}, {"items": ["<b>0–5 min:</b> Why it matters, and what the finished result looks like.", "<b>5–10 min:</b> Demonstrate at full speed, then slowly.", "<b>10–25 min:</b> Trainee practises. Give short feedback after each attempt.", "<b>25–30 min:</b> They do it once more on their own. Agree what to practise next."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Do it right in front of them. If you take a shortcut while demonstrating, that is the shortcut they will learn.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Common mistakes"}, {"items": ["Demonstrating too fast, or only once.", "Talking while they are trying to concentrate.", "Taking over the task when they struggle.", "Ending the session before they have done it alone."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Show it at full speed, then slowly, step by step.", "Most of the session is the trainee practising.", "End with them doing it alone."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754638147226, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 1, "title": "Skill", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790768209781336', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A module is a short, focused piece of training built around one objective. In Module 3 you broke a task into steps. Now you will turn those steps into a module a trainee can follow, and a trainer can deliver the same way every time, in every restaurant.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Rule 1: No clutter"}, {"html": "Only add content that helps the trainee reach the objective. Everything else is clutter, and clutter hides the important parts.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Ask of every slide, paragraph or video:</b> “Does the trainee need this to meet the objective?” If not, cut it.", "<b>One module, one objective.</b> If you are teaching two things, build two modules.", "<b>Keep the nice-to-know for later.</b> History, background and extra detail can go in a separate reading, not the core module."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Rule 2: Keep motivation a priority"}, {"html": "A trainee who is motivated learns faster and remembers more. Build motivation into the module rather than hoping it appears.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Start with why.</b> Open with how this task affects the guest, the team or the trainee themselves.", "<b>Show progress.</b> Short lessons and clear steps let people see themselves moving forward.", "<b>Make it relevant.</b> Use examples from our restaurant, our menu and our guests."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What every module should contain"}, {"items": ["<b>Title and objective.</b> “By the end of this module, you will be able to…”", "<b>Why it matters.</b> One or two sentences.", "<b>The steps.</b> From your breakdown, chunked and in order.", "<b>What good looks like.</b> The standard for each step.", "<b>Practice.</b> What the trainee does on the floor or in the kitchen.", "<b>Check.</b> A short quiz, an observation, or both.", "<b>Sign-off.</b> Who confirms the trainee meets the standard."], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790754647697178, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "Building The System", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790768329715947', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Before you publish a module, test it against three principles. If it fails any one of them, trainees will struggle and trainers will deliver it differently.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Principle 1: Simple"}, {"html": "Use the fewest steps and the plainest words that still meet the standard.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Step One: Smile<br>Step Two: Introduce Yourself<br>Step Three: Ask if it is their first time<br><br>Three Steps. Simple. Clear. Easy to remember. Easy to execute.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Use short sentences that start with a verb.", "Explain any jargon, or remove it."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Principle 2: Visual"}, {"html": "People remember what they see far better than what they read. Show the standard, do not only describe it.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Photos</b> of the finished plate, the set table or the clean station.", "<b>Short videos</b> of the task done to standard.", "<b>Colour codes and labels</b> that work in any language.", "<b>Before and after</b> images to show what to correct."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<i>Tip:</i> take photos in your own restaurants. Trainees should see our plates, our tables and our kitchen, not stock images.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Principle 3: Easy"}, {"html": "Make the module easy to use for both the trainee and the trainer.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Easy to find.</b> In iTrain, in the right course and chapter, with a clear title.", "<b>Easy to follow.</b> Short lessons, in the right order, with a time estimate.", "<b>Easy to deliver.</b> Any trainer can pick it up and teach it the same way.", "<b>Easy to check.</b> A clear standard and a short check at the end."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Quick check before you publish"}, {"items": ["Could a new hire follow it without help?", "Is there a photo or video for the key steps?", "Can any trainer deliver it the same way?", "Is it short enough to complete in one session?"], "t": "ol"}, {"items": ["Simple: few steps, plain words.", "Visual: show the standard with photos and video.", "Easy: easy to find, follow, deliver and check."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754647697178, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Three Principles to Keep in Mind: Simple, Visual, Easy", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790768434948123', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A good module only works if it is delivered, completed and checked. iTrain is where that happens. It turns training from something people say they did into something you can see.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "1. Builds accountability"}, {"items": ["<b>Assigned courses and modules.</b> Admins assign each learner their courses, and each trainer the modules they are responsible for.", "<b>Deadlines.</b> Every assignment can have a deadline, for the whole course and for individual chapters. Deadlines can be sent as Google Calendar invites.", "<b>Trainer sign-off.</b> Trainers tick off each lesson for a learner. iTrain records who signed it off and when.", "<b>Tests.</b> Learners must pass the course test to complete a course. Trainers can assign a test, or a retest to check what has been remembered."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "If it is not recorded, it did not happen. Sign off in iTrain after every session.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "2. Tracks progress"}, {"items": ["<b>Status for every learner.</b> Completed, delayed completion, overdue, in progress or not started.", "<b>Dashboard.</b> See progress across courses, learners and trainers, filtered by department.", "<b>Test results.</b> Best scores, pass rates and attempts for each learner and course.", "<b>Insights.</b> Activity, completion rates and where learners get stuck."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Use the dashboard before each shift or weekly meeting to see who is behind, and act early.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "3. Helps allocate time"}, {"items": ["<b>Lesson times.</b> Each lesson has a time estimate, and iTrain adds them up into the study time for each chapter and course.", "<b>Plan sessions.</b> Use these times to schedule training during quieter periods rather than in the middle of service.", "<b>Chapter deadlines.</b> Break a course into smaller targets so training is spread across the weeks, not left to the end.", "<b>Active time.</b> Insights show how long learners actually spend, so you can see whether the planned time is realistic."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Your routine as a trainer"}, {"items": ["Check your modules and deadlines on your iTrain home page.", "Plan your sessions using the lesson times.", "Deliver the training using the module.", "Sign off each lesson in iTrain once the standard is met.", "Assign the test when the learner is ready.", "Tell your manager early if anyone is falling behind."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Accountability: assignments, deadlines, sign-off and tests.", "Progress: statuses, the dashboard and insights.", "Time: lesson times, chapter deadlines and active time."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754647697178, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 2, "title": "How iTrain Can Help", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790769720386618', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Five things to remember about adult learners"}, {"items": ["<b>They want to know why.</b> “Do it this way” is weaker than “Do it this way because the guest with an allergy could be harmed.” Always give the reason.", "<b>They learn by doing.</b> Watching helps, but the skill sticks when they do the task themselves, with you beside them.", "<b>They bring experience.</b> Even a first-time hire has used a cash-drawer, served food or worked with others. Ask what they already know, and build on it.", "<b>They want it to be useful now.</b> Teach what they need for this shift first. Save the rest for later.", "<b>They protect their pride.</b> Nobody likes being corrected in front of guests or colleagues. Give feedback privately and keep it specific."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "The learning cycle-"}, {"items": ["<b>Tell.</b> Say what the task is, why it matters and what “good” looks like.", "<b>Show.</b> Demonstrate it at normal speed, then slowly, naming each step.", "<b>Do.</b> The trainee does it while you watch. Stay quiet unless there is a safety risk.", "<b>Review.</b> Ask what felt easy and what felt hard. Then give your feedback and repeat until it meets the standard."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "Watching is not training. A person has only learned a task when they can do it to standard without you.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Check understanding properly"}, {"html": "Many trainees nod to avoid looking slow. Do not ask “Does that make sense?” Instead:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["“Show me how you would do it.”", "“What would you do if the guest asked for X?”", "“Talk me through the steps in your own words.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Keep sessions short and spaced"}, {"items": ["Teach one task at a time. Three or four steps is plenty before practice.", "Short sessions repeated over several shifts work better than one long session.", "Return to earlier tasks to check they have stuck. Do not assume.", "Pick quiet periods. Do not train in the middle of a rush."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Give the reason, not just the rule.", "Let them do the task, with you beside them.", "Use the cycle: tell, show, do, review.", "Check understanding by asking them to show you."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754609397369, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "How Adults Learn on the Job", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790769743370588', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Someone new to the industry, or new to us, is often anxious. They worry about getting things wrong, slowing the team down and looking foolish. Your first job is to make them feel safe.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Before the first shift"}, {"items": ["Tell them what to expect: who they will meet, what they will do, when breaks are.", "Introduce them to the team by name and role.", "Show them where things are, such as lockers, toilets, the staff meal and the schedule.", "Give them one clear point of contact: you."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "During the first shifts"}, {"items": ["<b>Start with a small win.</b> Give a simple task they can do well early, so they feel useful.", "<b>One thing at a time.</b> Do not pile on information. Add the next skill once the first is steady.", "<b>Stay close.</b> Be visibly nearby, especially at first, and check in often.", "<b>Normalise mistakes.</b> Say, “Everyone gets this wrong at first. Here is what to do next time.”", "<b>Praise specifically.</b> “You checked the allergen before ringing it in. That is exactly right.” beats “Good job.”"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Say this, not that"}, {"items": ["<b>Instead of</b> “Just watch me and pick it up” <b>say</b> “I will show you, then you try, and I will be right here.”", "<b>Instead of</b> “That is wrong” <b>say</b> “Let us try that again. Here is the step to adjust.”", "<b>Instead of</b> “Why did you do that?” <b>say</b> “Talk me through what you were thinking.”", "<b>Instead of</b> “It is easy” <b>say</b> “It takes practice. You are doing fine.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Watch for signs they are struggling"}, {"items": ["Going quiet, or never asking questions.", "Repeating the same mistake after being shown.", "Avoiding guests or avoiding the kitchen line.", "Looking overwhelmed during busy periods."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "If you see these signs, slow down, simplify, and tell your manager early. Do not wait for the review.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Make them feel safe first. Skills follow.", "Give early wins and one task at a time.", "Correct kindly, privately and specifically.", "Tell your manager early if they are struggling."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754609397369, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 1, "title": "How to Train a Nervous First-Timer", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790769769270721', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Hiring someone with experience feels like a shortcut. But they arrive with habits from other workplaces. Some are fine. Some are shortcuts that put quality, safety or consistency at risk. They may also feel they do not need training. Handling this well protects the guest and respects the person.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Step 1: Do not assume. Observe."}, {"html": "Watch the person work before you correct anything. Note what they do well, as well as what differs from our standard. Start the conversation from respect: “You have a lot of experience. I would like to see how you work first.”", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Step 2: Sort what you see"}, {"items": ["<b>Standard.</b> Required, and affects guests, safety, quality or consistency.", "<b>Preference.</b> A different method that gives the same result.", "<b>Habit to avoid.</b> A shortcut that risks quality or safety."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Step 3: Correct with reasons"}, {"items": ["<b>Describe what you saw</b> without judging. “I noticed the 86 items were not uploaded.”", "<b>Explain the standard and why.</b> “Our standard is to ensure it is uploaded each time before service begins.”", "<b>Ask for their view.</b> “How did you do it at your last place?” Listen. You may learn something.", "<b>Agree the way forward.</b> “From today, we do it this way. Show me.”", "<b>Follow up.</b> Check again on the next shifts, and thank them when they get it right."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Handling pushback"}, {"items": ["<b>“That is how I have always done it.”</b> Respond: “I understand. Here, we do it this way, and here is why.”", "<b>“Our last place was better.”</b> Acknowledge it, then return to the standard: “Every place is different. This is ours.”", "<b>Keeps ignoring the standard.</b> Do not argue. Tell your manager. Holding the standard is not optional."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Respect their experience. Never lower the standard to keep the peace.", "t": "quote"}, {"html": "Experienced staff can help the team once they work to standard.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Observe first, then sort: standard, preference or habit.", "Always give the reason behind the standard.", "Allow harmless preferences. Correct unsafe habits every time.", "Escalate if the standard keeps being ignored."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754609397369, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 2, "title": "How to Train and Experienced Staff (With Bad Habits)", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790769805887934', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A trainee who does not speak up has not necessarily failed to understand. They may be working in a second language, or may be shy, worried about making mistakes, or used to a culture where questioning a senior person is not done. Your job is to make learning possible for everyone.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Language barriers"}, {"items": ["<b>Speak clearly and slowly.</b> Use short sentences and plain words. Avoid slang and kitchen jargon until you have explained it.", "<b>Show, do not only tell.</b> Demonstrate every step. Let them see it before they hear it.", "<b>Use visuals.</b> Photos, labels, plating guides and colour codes work in any language.", "<b>Name things.</b> Teach the key words for tools, ingredients and menu items, and repeat them often.", "<b>Check meaning.</b> Ask them to repeat the task in their own words, or to show you.", "<b>Allow help.</b> If a colleague shares their language, they can help explain, but you remain responsible for the standard."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Confidence barriers"}, {"items": ["<b>Ask open, low-pressure questions.</b> “Which part was very difficult?” is easier than “Do you understand?”", "<b>Give thinking time.</b> Wait a few seconds after a question. Do not fill the silence.", "<b>Praise effort and progress</b> in private first, then in front of the team when they are ready.", "<b>Do not put them on the spot</b> in front of guests or colleagues.", "<b>Offer a private route.</b> “If you are unsure about anything, find me after the shift.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Be careful about assumptions"}, {"items": ["Do not assume someone is slow because they speak little.", "Do not assume someone understands because they nod.", "Do not change the standard. Change the way you teach it."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Check understanding without embarrassment"}, {"items": ["Ask them to show you the task.", "Ask “What would you do if…?” using a real situation.", "Review again at the next shift, since memory fades."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Silence is not understanding, and not failure.", "Use visuals, demonstration and short, simple language.", "Make it safe to ask questions.", "Change how you teach, not the standard."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754609397369, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 3, "title": "Language and Confidence Barriers", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358021', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Welcome to the session on <b>Driving Business Acumen</b>. Driving business acumen is a logical and structured approach to problem solving, keeping in mind the strategic intent behind it. One of its most important parts is developing and sharpening <b>critical thinking</b>.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "In this module we cover critical thinking, problem-solving approaches and how to structure your thought process at a holistic level. We also look at the thought process and the key levers that drive our business, and work through some cases and problems together.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Business acumen is a logical, structured way of solving problems, always with the strategic intent in mind.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358014, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "Welcome to Driving Business Acumen", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358028', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Critical thinking brings together the capabilities that help you objectively analyse a situation and evaluate the pros, cons and implications of any course of action. These include, but are not limited to:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Strategic thinking", "Decision making", "Problem solving", "Risk assessment"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Build these skills and bring rigour and discipline to your thinking, and you will look at things from all angles. That makes you more likely to make good judgements, choices and decisions in every area of your work and life.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Start with the right attitude"}, {"items": ["<b>Open-mindedness</b>", "<b>Healthy scepticism</b>", "<b>Intellectual humility</b>", "<b>Free thinking</b>", "<b>High motivation</b>"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<b>Open-minded and sceptical</b> means seeking the facts, sources and reasoning behind an issue, examining it from as many sides as possible, and weighing the good and bad points of each, always aiming to get as close to the truth as you can. Too much scepticism leads you to doubt everything and commit to nothing; too little leads to gullibility.<br><br><b>Intellectual humility</b> means holding newly formed opinions loosely, being ready to examine new evidence even when it reveals flaws in your own beliefs, and thinking in “degrees of certainty” rather than right and wrong. Sometimes “I don’t know” is the wisest position.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "<b>Free thinking</b> means resisting the pressure to conform. Ask whether conformity is shaping your opinion, and have the courage to set a position aside until you have evaluated it properly.<br><br><b>High motivation</b> means being curious and willing to do the work: study, ask questions and reach a sufficient understanding before you make a judgement.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358014, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 1, "title": "Critical Thinking and the Right Attitude", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358035', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A proficient critical thinker works through three stages before making a decision.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "1. Intake"}, {"items": ["Gather the data and information that help you understand the problem or situation.", "Gather multiple perspectives to shape and add depth to your thinking.", "Sort out and narrow down what is important, separating <b>fact from opinion</b>.", "Identify assumptions and begin to question them."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "2. Investigate"}, {"items": ["Use a systematic approach to interpret and evaluate the information you have gathered.", "Judge the accuracy, clarity and relevance of your data.", "Keep challenging assumptions while avoiding emotional influences and biases."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "3. Act"}, {"items": ["Examine the implications of each possible course of action. Flow charts and diagrams help illustrate costs and benefits.", "What decision does the evidence support?", "What are the benefits and risks of making that decision?", "What is required to make that decision?"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358014, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 2, "title": "Intake, Investigate, Act", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358042', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Solving a business problem with a structured approach is one of the clearest signs of a good business manager. We face such problems almost every day, often under pressure and with an expectation of answers.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "A case is not about finding the right or wrong answer. It is about the method: the questions you raise, the assumptions you make, the issues you identify, the areas you prioritise, the frameworks you use, and the logical, confident recommendation you arrive at.", "t": "quote"}, {"html": "There are many ways to structure a problem. One useful approach is to look at every business from two sides: <b>inside the business</b> and <b>outside the business</b>, then break each into areas you can deep-dive into.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Inside the business"}, {"items": ["<b>Company &amp; capacity</b>: core competencies, brand and retail presence, reputation, financial, marketing and management resources", "<b>Revenue &amp; products</b>: substitutability, product line and features, pricing and volume, differentiators, premium pricing, distribution issues", "<b>Costs</b>: fixed costs, variable costs, deferrable or avoidable costs, allocation of overheads", "<b>Operations</b>: supplier issues, capacity and utilisation, throughput, bottlenecks, economies of scale and scope, quality issues"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Outside the business"}, {"items": ["<b>Market</b>: market size and share, capacity and growth, profitability, demand elasticity and market power, new entrants", "<b>Customers</b>: segmentation, wants and needs, how to satisfy them, what matters most to them, how much they will pay", "<b>Competition</b>: market penetration, strengths and weaknesses, how they meet market demand, costs and profitability, key advantages", "<b>Macro economy</b>: inflation, government regulation, international issues, unions, the value chain (where most value is created)"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358014, "content": "", "minutes": 12, "pos": 3, "title": "Structuring Your Thoughts", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358049', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Every company plans its financial year using the <b>Annual Planning Cycle</b>. It includes the company’s strategic plan, the annual operating plan and quarterly forecasting, which set the financial goals and targets. These cascade into region and district budgets and targets. Field reports, metrics and observations then track the current state against the targets, and as issues appear, DMs plan, do, check and adjust.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "Annual Planning Cycle overview: from the 5-year strategy to the P&L and scorecard", "src": "/uploads/361697330b3fa725384625c353ac163d.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"items": ["<b>Strategy</b> (5-year plan) leads to the <b>Annual Operating Plan</b>, including financial goals and targets for Q1.", "<b>Region / district budget and scorecard targets</b> are set. The current forecast is used for Q2, Q3 and Q4.", "<b>Tactical plan</b>: the DM creates the plan to meet the goals and targets.", "<b>Grasp the situation</b>: the business analysis cycle of reports, metrics and observations of behaviours.", "<b>Plan / Do / Check / Adjust</b>: coach, create a plan of action, or problem-solve.", "<b>Results</b>: the P&amp;L and scorecard."], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358014, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 4, "title": "The Annual Planning Cycle", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358063', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Labour is a strategic investment we make to deliver an engaging experience for our partners, exceptional customer service for our customers and financial benefit for our shareholders.", "t": "quote"}, {"html": "Two things matter most when managing labour: the overall business of the store, and the nature of the store.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "1. Overall business of the store"}, {"items": ["ADT (average daily transactions)", "AT (average ticket)", "Number of items being sold", "Peak and shoulder-peak ADT (day-part ADT)", "Weekend and weekday ADT mix"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "2. Nature of the store"}, {"items": ["Store type: single floor or two floors, cleaning needs", "Storage space: racks and chillers", "Key equipment: number of Mastrenas, ovens and POS machines", "Partner motion"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Together these give the store’s <b>IPLH</b> (items per labour hour), which tells you how many partners the store needs. You then schedule those partners around the same two factors.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358056, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "Labour as a Strategic Investment", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358070', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Once you know how many partners you need, build the schedule in this order:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Find the busiest and leanest days of the week. This tells you how many partners you need on each day.", "Map labour to the peak and shoulder-peak ADT curve. If 4–7 PM is the peak, have maximum labour from 3:30 PM to 7:30 PM. This spreads the peak further and grows the shoulder peak.", "Decide when you need part-timers, then capture staffing needs and hire.", "Use <b>Deployment 3.0</b> to deploy available partners in the most efficient way, removing bottlenecks and delivering amazing customer service.", "Map the schedule weekly and use the evaluation questions to check labour is managed efficiently."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "Labour is always used to grow transactions. If your store is achieving more than 100% of its ADT and items targets, plan to hire more labour to grow the business further.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358056, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Building the Schedule", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358077', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Evaluation"}, {"items": ["What scheduling problems have you identified, and what was the root cause? Look at forecasting accuracy, whether the schedule reflects the forecast, how it was executed, or a combination.", "How many times did you change the schedule after it was posted, and why?", "How many open shifts did you have? How could they have been prevented?", "How many baristas did you borrow after posting the schedule, and why?"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Planning & forecasting"}, {"items": ["What does successful planning and forecasting look like for you, and how do you measure it?", "How do you identify staffing needs and gaps by day and by day part?", "How many open shifts did you have over the past week or month, and why?", "How many baristas did you borrow, why did you need them, and where are your availability gaps?", "Knowing this, would you plan differently in future? How?", "What risk factors does your store face in maintaining staffing levels for current and future business?", "What system do you use to manage barista schedule requests?", "Where are you investing labour to grow transactions, and how do you manage it?"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358056, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 2, "title": "Evaluating Labour: Planning & Forecasting", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358084', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Scheduling"}, {"items": ["How do you know you have the right people scheduled at the right time?", "When did you give the schedule to your team? Did you change it after posting?", "How many of your regular customers know the baristas, and how many baristas know their regulars? How do they interact?", "How do you make sure baristas are scheduled for the right day part?", "Why did you adjust the schedule? What factors could you have considered when forecasting, and which could not be foreseen?"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Execution"}, {"items": ["What deployment problems have you recently identified?", "What did the DAS (Deployment Assessment Sheet) show?", "How did your store do on the 10 most common customer observations?", "Is deployment being flexed appropriately?", "How have you asked your team for feedback to identify problems?", "What countermeasures have you identified, and how can you implement them?", "What effect will solving these problems have for customers, baristas and the business?"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358056, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 3, "title": "Evaluating Labour: Scheduling & Execution", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358098', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "When inventory is managed effectively, sales and profitability increase and waste is minimised. Inventory is managed in four phases:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Set expectations</b>: the inventory management vision, across customer service, store organisation, partner experience and business.", "<b>Gather and analyse information</b>: reports, visual indicators and store observations.", "<b>Determine the level of impact</b>: your circle of focus, circle of influence and circle of concern.", "<b>Provide support</b>: coach, create an action plan, problem-solve."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "Inventory management vision: the right product, in the right place, at the right time to optimise sales and profit.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358091, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "The Four Phases of Inventory Management", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358105', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Use the vision to set expectations in four areas.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Customer service"}, {"items": ["Customers always get what they want: we always have the right product when they want it.", "A customer never has to ask, “Do you have…?”", "Partners never leave a customer to go and locate an item."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Store organisation"}, {"items": ["Product is kept at the point of use and the point of stock: no more than two places.", "Product is stored where it is easy to count and reach for every partner.", "All partners understand, and are accountable for, maintaining storage locations."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Partner experience and business"}, {"items": ["Partners understand how their role in inventory benefits the customer.", "“I don’t have to transfer, search for product, or tell my customers no anymore.”", "“I can run my routines and connect with customers without being interrupted by non-value-added inventory work.”", "Problem solving is part of our inventory culture, and the experience is consistent from store to store.", "<b>Business</b>: our practices support the operating model; sales keep growing (IPT, USD) and profit keeps improving through reduced waste."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358091, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Setting Expectations", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358112', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "The next step is to gather information, through a detailed store visit or by observing behaviour in the store using visual indicators and market tools.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Start with reports.</b> Analyse them during the business analysis cycle. The P&amp;L includes inventory adjustment information (COGS in-depth analysis).", "<b>Observe visual indicators</b> during a walk-through or PPV. The next lesson lists what to look for.", "<b>Observe and ask.</b> If you still need to understand the issue, watch partners managing inventory and ask them questions."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "Managing inventory levels is critical for your store and needs robust planning to deliver.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358091, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 2, "title": "Gathering and Analysing Information", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358119', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Food case"}, {"items": ["The food case is stocked with products right for the time of day, and every item on the store’s food menu is available.", "Residual food (out of case) is enough to support sales for the rest of the day, with enough back stock for demand.", "Food is clearly dated to the correct shelf life and rotated first in, first out. Multi-day items carry a day dot or sticker.", "The food case schematic is used, and the display is attractive."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<b>If not, research the root cause:</b> is product received accurately and receipts adjusted? Are counts accurate and variances corrected? Are markouts done correctly on the right day? Are transfers correct? Are partners ringing all food? Are orders adjusted to business need? Are date stickers available?", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Retail and front of house"}, {"items": ["Retail SKUs are fully stocked on wall bays and fixtures, per local marketing materials, with minimal excess stored.", "Each product is stored in one location, with similar items, close to its display fixture, so restocking and counting are quick.", "Whole bean coffee is stored in a single location and available to meet demand.", "Recipe items (syrups, mocha, toppings) are stored in no more than two places, close to the production area. Only approved containers are on counters."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Back of house"}, {"items": ["Obsolete inventory, trim and promotional merchandise are removed, and storage space fits current needs.", "Usage is tracked for basic suggested-order items, using 2–4 weeks of usage to set true needs.", "Pars are based on recent, accurate usage, clearly displayed and referred to when ordering.", "Shelving fits usage and pars, each item has one location, and FIFO rotation is followed.", "Delivery-readiness procedures are followed, and personal belongings are kept away from consumables and small wares."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358091, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 3, "title": "Visual Indicators: What Good Looks Like", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358133', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "These are the metrics used to read and drive a store’s business. The following lessons explain each one.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Transactions</b>: total transactions and ADT", "<b>Net sales</b>: total net value (revenue) and volume, and AT", "<b>USD</b>: units per store per day sold", "<b>IPT</b>: items sold per transaction", "<b>APR</b>: average price realisation, the average amount realised per unit sold", "<b>Comps</b>: same-store, same-period growth", "<b>Costs and margin</b>: COGS and net sales value", "<b>Product mix</b>: menu mix and size mix", "<b>People metrics</b>: annualised attrition, IPLH, part-timer %, diversity %", "<b>Loyalty</b>: MSR tender and activations per day"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Growing transactions is the key to long-term success.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 0, "title": "The Metrics That Matter", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358140', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Transactions and ADT"}, {"items": ["Every ring on the POS, or every time money changes hands, across all stores = <b>total transactions</b>.", "One transaction can include several products, and cannot be assigned to a specific product or category.", "Transactions indicate how many customers come into our stores. We generally assume every transaction includes at least one beverage.", "Affected by the number of customers visiting (economy, competitors) and the number of stores opened or closed."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Net sales"}, {"items": ["<b>Net sales units</b>: the total units sold in a given timeframe.", "<b>Net sales (revenue)</b>: the overall income in a timeframe, net of returns and taxes.", "Our measure is <b>AT (average ticket)</b> = total net revenue ÷ total transactions."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Net sales are driven by <b>total transactions</b> (more transactions mean more revenue, all else equal) and by <b>average ticket</b>. Items per transaction is <b>IPT = items sold ÷ transactions</b>, and <b>AT = APR × IPT</b>.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Transactions, ADT and Net Sales", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358147', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "<b>Average ticket (AT)</b> is the price a customer pays per transaction. It depends on <b>APR</b> (average price realisation) and <b>IPT</b> (items per transaction).<br><br><b>APR = total value of products sold ÷ total volume of products sold.</b> Sell five beverages as three cappuccinos and two lattes, then as two flat whites, two vanilla lattes and a cappuccino, and the second mix has the higher beverage APR.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to lift APR"}, {"items": ["<b>Sell more LTOs</b>: they move APR, and so AT.", "<b>Recommend higher-value items in the same bucket</b>: Cold Coffee to Coffee Frappuccino, a flat white instead of a latte, a Cocoa Cappuccino instead of a Cappuccino.", "<b>Introduce new items</b> priced higher.", "<b>Upgrade sizes</b>: a Tall gives a higher APR than a Short.", "Watch out: adding items priced below the category’s average ticket lowers the category ticket."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Worked example"}, {"html": "<b>Scenario 1:</b> 1 Cappuccino (Rs 155), 1 Latte (Rs 155), 2 Java Chip Frappuccinos (Rs 210 each), 1 Coffee Frappuccino (Rs 180).<br>APR = (155 + 155 + 420 + 180) ÷ 5 = <b>Rs 182</b><br><br><b>Scenario 2:</b> 1 Valencia Orange Cappuccino (Rs 265), 1 Latte (Rs 155), 2 Java Chip Frappuccinos (Rs 210 each), 1 Cold Coffee (Rs 230).<br>APR = (265 + 155 + 420 + 230) ÷ 5 = <b>Rs 214</b>", "t": "p"}, {"html": "AT build-up: a customer buying one beverage at Rs 150 and one food item at Rs 125 has an IPT of 2 and an AT of Rs 275. AT moves through IPT and APR: price increases, higher-priced new items and size upgrades or downgrades all change it.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 2, "title": "Average Ticket and APR", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358154', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "USD: units per store per day"}, {"items": ["Measures daily store unit sales volume and shows absolute (not relative) size.", "USD = net sales units ÷ active store days. It highlights food unit volume: sell 12 sandwiches from the case today and that is 12 USD.", "Affected by transactions (fewer customers, fewer units) and IPT (fewer items attached, lower USD)."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Growing food IPT"}, {"items": ["Food IPT = food units sold ÷ transactions: a measure of attachment.", "Know what sells in each day part: breakfast (till 12 noon), lunch (till 4 PM), evening (till 10 PM), post 10 PM.", "Display food effectively: how are your top 10 items displayed? Rotate displays to boost sales.", "Keep ordering (sub-category) availability right, explore bulk orders, use surgical sampling, and build partners’ product knowledge."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Growing merchandise IPT"}, {"items": ["Every shelf has a Rs sales per sq ft. What are your top two merchandise items, and how many do you sell daily?", "Where are they placed? Are they in the highest sales-per-sq-ft spots: the wall bay, the feature cube, the coffee floor basket?", "What do you want to sell? Why is that merchandise, whole bean or VIA placed there? Has it helped sales, or do you need to change it?"], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 3, "title": "USD and Growing IPT", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358161', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Comps (comparable store sales)"}, {"items": ["Measures same-store sales growth against the previous year, and predicts total store sales.", "<b>Revenue comp</b> = net sales value ÷ net sales value last year, only for stores at least 12 months old.", "<b>ADT comp</b> = ADT for this period ÷ ADT for the same period last year."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to drive ADT comp"}, {"items": ["Enhance service levels: grow the <b>peak period</b> and expand the <b>shoulder peak</b>.", "Grow loyalty.", "Generate trials and drive new products.", "Run effective LSM, leveraging the trade area, occasions, events and festivals."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Category mix"}, {"items": ["Mix is the share of the “pie” within a category or group of products, by sales value or sales volume.", "Mix % = units ÷ total units, or sales ÷ total sales.", "Changes in mix affect ticket, sales, costs and gross margin. A customer switching from a sandwich to a cookie loses revenue and margin in absolute terms."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 4, "title": "Comps and Category Mix", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358168', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Annualised attrition"}, {"html": "Annualised YTD attrition = (total separations ÷ average headcount) × 12 ÷ months gone by.<br>Example: 3 separations over 5 months with an average headcount of 12.3 gives (3 ÷ 12.3) × 12 ÷ 5 = <b>58.5%</b>.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "IPLH (items per labour hour)"}, {"html": "IPLH = items ÷ labour hours.<br><b>Items</b> = food, beverage, merchandise and whole bean sold, plus partner markouts (not modifiers, sampling or other markouts).<br><b>Labour hours</b> include overtime and straight time, excluding leave and break hours.<br>Example: 141,457 items over 16,865 labour hours gives a YTD IPLH of <b>8.39</b>.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Part-timer % and diversity %"}, {"html": "<b>PT %</b> = part-time baristas ÷ (part-time + full-time baristas) on a given date. Six part-timers and eight full-timers give 6 ÷ 14 = <b>42.85%</b>.<br><br><b>Diversity %</b> = women partners ÷ (male + female partners) on a given date, counting each part-timer as 1.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 5, "title": "People Metrics", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358175', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "<b>MSR tender</b> is the percentage of net sales paid with a Starbucks Card. It has three components: unregistered cards, registered cards and the mobile app. If a store’s daily sales are Rs 50,000 and MSR tender is 40%, then Rs 20,000 was paid by Starbucks Card.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Registered My Starbucks Rewards members visit more often than customers without a card.", "It is easier and cheaper to reach registered members through mailers, SMS and the app.", "Higher MSR tender means more repeat customers and a better chance of ADT comp growth.", "On average, a <b>Gold</b> member visits 6–8 times a month, a <b>Green</b> member 3–4 times and a <b>Welcome</b> member 0.5–2 times."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<b>Activations per day</b> is the other key metric. Unless new cards are activated, the loyalty base cannot grow, so activating new Starbucks Cards at store level is critical.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Three basics every partner should know"}, {"items": ["MSR is a <b>prepaid card</b>: the customer loads money onto it.", "Activate a card with Rs 200 in store and register it online.", "Load money and use it for purchases. A customer earns 1 star for every Rs 300 spent.", "There are three levels based on a year’s spend, each with different rewards, plus extra rewards during promotions."], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 6, "title": "Loyalty: MSR Tender and Activations", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358182', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Average daily sales come down to two numbers: <b>ADS = ADT × AT</b>. Every metric in this chapter feeds one of them, and every action you take in store is an input to one of those metrics.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "Key levers: the variables behind ADT and AT, and the store inputs that drive them", "src": "/uploads/3c7cb9f941b4d3529e213411fc3042db.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"items": ["<b>ADT variables</b>: capacity utilisation (peak period + shoulder peak), loyalty (MSR tender % + activations per day), new customers (generating trials through LSM + new product drive), all supported by CV scores.", "<b>AT variables</b>: IPT (beverage ~ group size, food ~ day part, merchandise, RTD&amp;E) and APR (LTOs, size, upsell).", "<b>Capacity inputs</b>: staffing and scheduling, Deployment 3.0 (DAS), role clarity and 5S, training at stores, SM approach and lead.", "<b>Loyalty inputs</b>: programme knowledge, talking points, customer service, going above and beyond, SM approach and lead.", "<b>New customer inputs</b>: PPG planning, what success looks like and ROI, execution and tracking, 1:1 connect, SM approach and lead.", "<b>AT inputs</b>: PPG detailing, projection and follow-up, unique selling point of the product, ordering and inventory management, generating trial, category knowledge, merchandising and display, My Mastrena, SM approach and lead."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358126, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 7, "title": "Key Levers: How It All Connects", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358196', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Below is one week’s schedule for a high street (HS) store in Mumbai with 15 partners. The store is open from 7 AM to 11:30 PM every day and is a single-floor store. Use the lessons on labour management to analyse it.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "Weekly schedule for partners P1–P15 (start and end times; 0:00–0:00 means a day off)", "src": "/uploads/a8cbe0cde99d07fcc709115acf21733c.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "Estimated day-part ADT for the week", "src": "/uploads/d9be627bfef03f5770b4e710297d1810.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "Hourly IPT by category", "src": "/uploads/0990b0b5120815e555f56a5cfc3fae6e.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "Other information: weekly peak-hour maximum transactions against the all-time maximum of 74", "src": "/uploads/56b04598361d5ad2c2cdc52a4f94ab7f.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Questions"}, {"items": ["Do you feel the schedule is rightly made? How will you structure your thoughts?", "Are there any opportunities? List them.", "If you had to redraw the schedule, what changes would you make?", "Which slot is ideal for part-timers, and how many part-timers would you have?"], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358189, "content": "", "minutes": 25, "pos": 0, "title": "Case Study 1: Staffing & Scheduling", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358203', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Below are the average ticket details of a high street store for FY17 and FY18: AT, average price realisation, items per transaction, drink size mix and ADT.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "AT, APR and IPT by department, drink size mix (% quantity) and ADT, FY17 against FY18", "src": "/uploads/eda09df5f4bc91bead04c3b9209d5903.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "How would you approach looking at how AT is behaving and moving?", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["What aspects would you look at?", "How would you factor in and link those aspects?", "How would you prioritise the steps you want the team to take?"], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358189, "content": "", "minutes": 20, "pos": 1, "title": "Case Study 2: Average Ticket", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358210', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A mall store doing around <b>350 ADT</b> has the following loyalty, transaction and customer voice (CV) data.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "MSR tender %, ADT and activations per day, FY17 against FY18", "src": "/uploads/910eade85f0844ce80a2fcd29f620e07.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "CV scores: Get to Know Me and Speed of Service", "src": "/uploads/369d901e82e2bc70fe0a6b35f9e158e0.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "CV scores: Go Above & Beyond, Order Accuracy and Beverage Taste", "src": "/uploads/c78c5502f3768a719701dcdf545cb480.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "Peak-period ADT movement by week against the all-time maximum of 115", "src": "/uploads/bd00bf1971558001040600f8e3321e7e.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "<b>Customer comments (CV):</b><br>“This visit was good. The coffee I had was great, worth the money spent.”<br>“Please get the wild mushroom pockets back. I miss it.”<br>“The staff member Tampa was very helpful and professional.”<br>“Try and make hot cappuccino strong.” / “I keep wanting a stronger Americano. The shots in Grande seem less to me.” / “Make the black coffee taste stronger.”<br>“There is always a long queue to place the order. Increase the variety in iced tea.”<br>“Prices are too high and there is no lemon loaf cake.”<br>“The tables should be cleaned quickly after the customer leaves.”<br><br><b>Store team:</b> SM stability 2.25 years. Shift supervisors: 7, 15, 18 and 3 months. 16 partners in total (4 part-time), diversity 25%.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["What inferences do you draw from this information?", "Based on it, what are the key steps and action plan to drive and enhance loyalty at the store?"], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358189, "content": "", "minutes": 25, "pos": 2, "title": "Case Study 3: Loyalty", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358217', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A high street store in a residential catchment is in its fourth year of operation. The trade area has many housing societies, 4 colleges, 3 schools and 2 club houses.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["75 seats; around 410 ADT across the year: about 375 on weekdays and 500 at weekends.", "Customers: youngsters, business men and women, families, and homemakers (kitty parties).", "Day-part mix: morning (7 AM–12 noon) 15%, afternoon (12–4 PM) 25%, evening (4–8 PM) 35%, night (8 PM–1 AM) 25%.", "Top beverages: Latte, Java Chip Frappuccino, Cappuccino. Top food: Chatpata Paratha Wrap, Red Velvet Cake, Egg White Chicken Ham.", "LTO conversion around 18–20%; MSR tender 30%.", "ADT comp +4%; revenue comp +9%."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Your task"}, {"html": "Using this information and your knowledge of September’s activities and promotions (all promotions running from 11 September apply to this store), choose a tactic from the tactic library to drive ADT and revenue. Which tactic would you choose, and why? What factors would you consider when selecting it?", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358189, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 3, "title": "Case Study 4: LSM Selection", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358231', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "<b>PrescriptCo</b> is a pharmaceutical company that delivers high-end drugs to patients with chronic diseases, using custom packaging and an overnight delivery company. Its CFO hired a consultant, <b>Davis Robichard</b>, to cut expenses. His report says shipping and handling costs are excessive and recommends <b>stopping weekend deliveries</b>.<br><br>You are the head of PrescriptCo’s Logistics Management Department. Your boss, <b>Stan Blakely</b>, Director of Operations, has asked you to evaluate the situation and the consultant’s recommendation. Use the <b>Intake, Investigate, Act</b> approach from Chapter 1.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "PrescriptCo’s mission: to put our customers’ needs first by making it as easy as possible for them to obtain the high-quality medications they need when they need them.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Discovery zone: the data"}, {"items": ["<b>Consultant’s report</b>: delivery costs were over 15% of operating expenses last year, and last quarter’s were about 25% higher than the quarter before. Weekend deliveries are blamed for the increase.", "<b>Quarterly shipping costs</b>: Q1 $770,000, Q2 $710,000, Q3 $630,000, Q4 $880,000.", "<b>Home delivery claims processed</b>: Q1 13.1M, Q2 12.8M, Q3 12.4M, Q4 15.2M.", "<b>Quarterly special packaging costs</b>: Q1 $225,000, Q2 $280,000, Q3 $260,000, Q4 $341,000.", "<b>Delivery contract</b> (corporate attorney): the flat quarterly rate covers up to 15,000 weekend deliveries, then $50 per extra delivery. Before last quarter they never came within 1,000 of the limit; last quarter there were about 16,500, costing almost $75,000 in surcharges."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What people are saying"}, {"items": ["<b>Stan Blakely</b>: there is a big push to cut costs and the report has drawn a lot of interest; he needs your view as soon as possible.", "<b>Pauline French</b> (logistics analyst) has benchmarking studies, competitive comparisons and customer surveys ready.", "<b>Davis Robichard</b>: PrescriptCo doesn’t take new orders at weekends, so dropping weekend delivery is a “win-win”; his report is “unbiased and data supported”, and looking at the raw data would be “wasting your time”.", "<b>Brian Bates</b> (shipping specialist): 44% of customers are under 65, against about a third at competitors. Packaging costs rose over $40,000 last quarter due to demand for orphan drugs, though industry talk says those costs are trending down.", "<b>Articles</b>: many new specialty drugs need less special handling, such as no refrigeration. Orphan drug demand is rising, boosted short-term by a recent viral outbreak, and next-generation orphan drugs are dropping special handling requirements."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358224, "content": "", "minutes": 40, "pos": 0, "title": "Home Assignment 1: PrescriptCo (Critical Thinking)", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790777616358238', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A mall store with these details: <b>3,000 sq ft</b>, <b>28 tables</b>, <b>64 chairs</b>, open for <b>4 years</b>.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "ADT and AT by year, IPT by category (FY13–FY18), and monthly AT, ADT and IPT analysis", "src": "/uploads/aaed1e17c344c4dd9b35c2e006740b30.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"caption": "MSR tender, activations per day and LTO conversion by month, FY17 against FY18", "src": "/uploads/ad5c4442bada000b4395730f4b5846e9.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "<b>MSR tender by year:</b> FY15 11%, FY16 18%, FY17 26%, FY18 22%.<br><b>LSM initiatives so far:</b> Q1 and Q2, Rs 50 off a second service, used in mall events.<br><br><b>Partner details:</b> SM stability 1.9 years (Sachin). Attrition 30%. Diversity 22%. 17 effective partners (20% part-time). 4 shift supervisors and 1 ASM; shift supervisor tenure from 2 to 9 months.", "t": "p"}, {"caption": "Partner roles by system (New, L, O, A)", "src": "/uploads/0531168e6db9332329c717dfc7e1ce64.jpg", "t": "img"}, {"html": "<b>Customer Connection score</b> (CV, last six months): 42, 42, 45, 48, 43, 50, 52.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "<b>Your task:</b> using all of this information, recommend <b>three tiered priorities</b> for the store to drive ADT and revenue growth, with your reasons for choosing each. Then propose and explain the LSM you would run in Q3.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790777616358224, "content": "", "minutes": 40, "pos": 1, "title": "Home Assignment 2: Store Priorities & LSM", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790840040456007', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Think about the last time a guest had a great experience with us. Now think about the last time a guest had a poor one. In most cases, the difference was not the menu or the room. It was whether the person serving or cooking knew exactly what to do, and had been shown properly.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "1. Guests experience our training"}, {"html": "Guests never see our SOPs, checklists or training records. They see the result: how they are greeted, how accurately their order is taken, how the food looks and tastes, and how smoothly service flows. A well-trained team is consistent: the same quality on a Tuesday lunch as on a Saturday night, and from one team member to the next.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Inconsistency is one of the most common sources of guest complaints. When a guest says “It was better last time,” they are describing a training and standards gap.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "2. Training and the team"}, {"html": "New hires decide quickly whether they feel supported. A poor first week, left to watch, guess or learn by making mistakes in front of guests, often leads to an early exit. Early turnover is expensive:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Time spent recruiting and interviewing again.", "Lost productivity while a new person learns the role.", "Extra strain on the rest of the team, who cover the gaps."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Staff who are trained well are more confident, make fewer mistakes and need less supervision.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "3. Training and the business"}, {"items": ["<b>Quality and cost:</b> waste, remakes and errors fall when people are trained to standard.", "<b>Efficiency:</b> speed and service flow improve when everyone works the same way.", "<b>Brand:</b> consistency across the restaurant protects the guest’s trust."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "4. Your part as a trainer"}, {"html": "Every new hire’s first impression of “how we do things” comes from their trainer. You are not just passing on tasks. You are passing on standards, attitude and pride in the work.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Guests experience the quality of our training.", "Good training reduces early turnover and mistakes.", "Consistency across all locations starts with trainers."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Reflect and apply"}, {"items": ["Recall a time a guest was unhappy because something was inconsistent. What was missing in the training?", "Think about your own first week here. What helped you feel supported, and what was missing?", "What is one thing you want every new hire you train to say about their first week?"], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790840040456000, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "Why Training Drives Guest Experience and Retention", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790840040456017', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Becoming a trainer changes your role. You are often still a colleague and sometimes a supervisor, but when you train, you have a different job.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A trainer…"}, {"items": ["Teaches the standard clearly and patiently.", "Demonstrates, observes and gives feedback.", "Checks that learning has happened, not just that time has passed.", "Reports progress honestly."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A trainer is not…"}, {"items": ["Someone who just says “follow me and watch.” Watching is not training.", "Someone who teaches shortcuts or personal habits.", "Someone who does the trainee’s work to save time.", "Someone who criticises in front of guests or the team."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Common challenges"}, {"items": ["<b>Time pressure:</b> plan training for quieter periods and tell the trainee when you will return to the topic.", "<b>Friendship:</b> stay kind, but keep the standard the same for everyone.", "<b>Being the expert:</b> experts skip steps that feel obvious. A new hire needs every step."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790840040456000, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 1, "title": "What a Trainer Is (and Isn't)", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790840040456024', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Every experienced team member develops their own way of doing things. Some habits are harmless, some are better than the official method, and some break standards. A trainer must know which is which and teach only the house standard.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Three categories"}, {"items": ["<b>Standard:</b> required. Affects guests, safety, quality or consistency. FOH: greeting within the agreed time, allergen check at order. BOH: colour-coded knives and boards, temperature checks.", "<b>Preference:</b> a personal method that gives the same result. FOH: the order you set a table. BOH: how you arrange your station within the rules. <i>Trainer action: allow it, but the result must be the same.</i>", "<b>Habit to avoid:</b> a shortcut that risks quality or safety. FOH: carrying too many plates to save a trip. BOH: skipping a label or date check “because it’s quiet.” <i>Trainer action: do not pass it on. Correct it.</i>"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Activity: sort the practice"}, {"html": "For each statement, decide: Standard, Preference or Habit to avoid. Answers are listed below.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["“I always fold napkins my way, but they look the same as the house style.”", "“We skip the allergen question when the guest looks regular.”", "“Bread and water go to the table within the agreed time.”", "“I like to prep my garnishes at the start of the shift rather than the end.”", "“It’s fine to taste with the same spoon if you wipe it.”"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Answers"}, {"items": ["<b>Preference.</b> The result matches the standard, so the method is flexible.", "<b>Habit to avoid.</b> Standards apply to every guest, every time.", "<b>Standard.</b> A measurable house requirement.", "<b>Preference.</b> If quality and food-safety rules are met, timing is flexible.", "<b>Habit to avoid.</b> Hygiene standards must be followed exactly."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "If you are not sure whether something is a standard or a preference, check the SOP or ask your manager. Don’t guess, and don’t teach it as a rule until you know.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790840040456000, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 2, "title": "Standards vs. Personal Style", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790840040456033', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Your responsibilities"}, {"items": ["<b>Teach to the standard.</b> Use approved SOPs, checklists and recipes, not memory or habit.", "<b>Plan the training.</b> Know what you will cover, how long it takes and what the trainee should be able to do afterwards.", "<b>Observe and give feedback.</b> Say clearly what went well and what to improve.", "<b>Assess honestly.</b> Sign off only when the trainee meets the standard.", "<b>Keep records.</b> Update the training tracker after each session.", "<b>Model the behaviour.</b> Trainees copy what you do, not just what you say.", "<b>Escalate concerns.</b> Tell your manager early if a trainee is struggling, the standard is unclear, or there is a safety or welfare issue."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "You can"}, {"items": ["Decide whether a trainee is ready for the next step.", "Hold back a sign-off if the standard isn’t met.", "Pause training during peak service.", "Request extra practice time."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Check with your manager first"}, {"items": ["Extending or shortening the agreed training plan.", "Ending a trainee’s training or confirming a probation outcome.", "Changing the SOP or the standard."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Standards for trainer conduct"}, {"items": ["Treat every trainee with respect, whatever their age, background or experience.", "Give feedback privately wherever possible.", "Keep trainee information confidential.", "If something feels unsafe or unfair, stop and speak to your manager."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790840040456000, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 3, "title": "Your Responsibilities and Authority as a Trainer", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790840040456040', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Rate yourself honestly on each statement. There are no right or wrong answers. This is for your development and will be shared only with your manager and course lead.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "<b>Scale:</b> 1 = Not yet confident, 2 = Developing, 3 = Confident, 4 = Very confident", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["I can explain a task clearly, step by step, to someone who has never done it.", "I stay patient when someone makes the same mistake more than once.", "I give feedback that is specific, not vague.", "I can tell when someone has understood and when they’re just nodding.", "I follow the house standard even under pressure.", "I’m comfortable correcting a colleague, or someone more senior than me.", "I can adapt my approach to different people.", "I keep records and follow through on what I say I’ll do."], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790840040456000, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 4, "title": "Your Strengths as a Trainer", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790841000000100', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "As a trainer you do two different jobs. Teaching gives someone knowledge and skills they do not have yet. Coaching helps someone use what they already know, and do it better. Mixing them up is one of the most common reasons training stalls.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Teaching: you show, they learn the standard"}, {"html": "Teaching is for new knowledge and new skills. You are the expert, and you decide what is covered and in what order.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["You explain, demonstrate and give the right answer.", "You teach the house standard, exactly as written in the SOP.", "Best for new hires, new menu items, safety and hygiene rules, anything that has one correct method."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "FOH example: showing a new server the allergen check at the point of order, step by step. BOH example: showing a new cook the knife and board colour codes.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Coaching: you ask, they work it out"}, {"html": "Coaching is for someone who already knows the basics. You are guiding their thinking, not giving them the answer.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["You ask questions, observe, and give feedback on what you saw.", "The focus is how they work now and how to improve it.", "Best for building speed, confidence and judgment, fixing habits, and preparing someone for more responsibility."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "FOH example: after a busy shift, asking a server, “What would you do differently with table 12?” BOH example: asking a cook, “What slowed you down on that ticket, and what would help?”", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Teaching and coaching side by side"}, {"items": ["Teaching assumes they do not know. Coaching assumes they know the basics.", "Teaching, you are the expert. Coaching, you are the guide.", "Teaching, explain and demonstrate. Coaching, ask questions and give feedback.", "Teaching, you do. Coaching, they do.", "Teaching, the trainee can do the task to standard. Coaching, the trainee does it better, faster and more independently.", "Teaching is done when showing the module. Coaching is done post to maintain standard of what was taught."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Teach the standard. Coach the performance. If you coach before the person has been taught, you are asking them to guess.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Moving from teaching to coaching"}, {"html": "A good trainer gradually hands control to the trainee. A simple sequence for any task:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Tell and show.</b> Explain the task and demonstrate it to standard.", "<b>Do it together.</b> They try, with you beside them.", "<b>Watch.</b> They do it alone while you observe without stepping in.", "<b>Coach.</b> Ask: “What went well? What would you change?” Add your own feedback after they answer.", "<b>Sign off.</b> Only when they meet the standard on their own."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Common mistakes"}, {"items": ["<b>Coaching too early.</b> Asking “What do you think you should do?” on day one.", "<b>Teaching too long.</b> Still giving instructions to someone who is ready to work it out.", "<b>Telling instead of asking.</b> Jumping in with the answer before they have had a chance to think.", "<b>Letting standards slide.</b> Using “let them figure it out” for something that must be done one way."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754638147226, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 2, "title": "Teaching vs. Coaching", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000300', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Experienced people do their job without thinking about the steps. That is exactly the problem. When you teach a task you know well, you skip steps that feel obvious, and a new hire is left guessing. Breaking a task down means writing out every step, in order, so nothing is missed.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What goes wrong without a breakdown"}, {"items": ["You forget a step, so the trainee learns it wrong.", "Two trainers teach the same task two different ways.", "The trainee feels overwhelmed and stops asking questions.", "You cannot tell what they have learned, because you did not define what “done” looks like."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What a breakdown gives you"}, {"items": ["<b>Clarity.</b> The trainee sees what they will learn and in what order.", "<b>Consistency.</b> Every trainer teaches the same steps, in every restaurant.", "<b>Confidence.</b> Small steps build small wins."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "If you cannot write the task as steps, you are not ready to teach it yet.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Experts skip steps without noticing. A breakdown stops that.", "A good breakdown makes training clear, consistent and measurable."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 0, "title": "Why Break a Task Down", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000307', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Before you list the steps, decide what the trainee should be able to do at the end. This is the objective. Every step you teach should lead towards it.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A good objective is…"}, {"items": ["<b>Specific.</b> One task, not a whole job.", "<b>Observable.</b> You can see them doing it.", "<b>Measurable.</b> There is a standard to meet, such as a time, a temperature or a checklist.", "<b>Realistic.</b> Achievable in the time you have."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Weak and strong objectives"}, {"items": ["<b>Weak:</b> “Understand how to greet guests.” You cannot see understanding.", "<b>Strong:</b> “Greet every guest within the agreed time, introduce yourself and ask if it is their first visit.”", "<b>Weak:</b> “Know the allergen process.”", "<b>Strong:</b> “Ask every guest about allergies at the point of order, record the answer on the ticket and confirm it with the kitchen.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "A useful way to start is the sentence: <b>“By the end of this session, the trainee will be able to…”</b> followed by an action word such as greet, prepare, check, record, serve or clean.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "From objective to steps"}, {"items": ["Write the objective in one sentence.", "Do the task yourself and note each action, as you actually do it.", "Write the steps as short instructions starting with a verb.", "Test it: could a new hire follow these steps without you?", "Fix any gaps, then check it against the SOP."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Start with what the trainee will be able to do.", "Make the objective specific, observable and measurable.", "Then list the steps that lead to it."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 1, "title": "Start with the Objective", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000314', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Every task needs some mix of three things. Knowing which is which helps you pick the right training method.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "1. Knowledge: what they need to know"}, {"items": ["Menu items, ingredients, allergens, prices, opening hours, SOP rules.", "Teach it by explaining, showing, and checking with questions."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "2. Skill: what they need to be able to do"}, {"items": ["Carrying food, using the salver, clearance, plating, making a coffee.", "Teach it by demonstrating, then letting them practise. Skill needs repetition and feedback."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "3. Attitude: how they behave"}, {"items": ["Warmth, attention to detail, pride in the work.", "Teach it by modelling it yourself, and by recognising it when you see it."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Example: serving a table"}, {"items": ["<b>Knowledge:</b> the menu, allergens, table numbers.", "<b>Skill:</b> carrying plates, clearing, using the system.", "<b>Attitude:</b> making eye contact, smiling, noticing when a guest needs something."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Many trainers teach knowledge and skill but forget attitude. Yet guests remember how they were treated. Include it in the breakdown, with observable behaviours such as “makes eye contact and smiles”.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 2, "title": "Knowledge, Skill and Attitude", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000321', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Once you have your steps, the order and size matter. Too many steps at once overwhelm a learner. Steps in the wrong order cause confusion.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Chunk it"}, {"items": ["Group steps into chunks of about three to five.", "Teach one chunk, practise it, then move to the next.", "Name each chunk so the trainee can remember it."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Order it"}, {"items": ["<b>Follow the real sequence</b> in which the task happens.", "<b>Start simple.</b> Begin with the easiest chunk to give an early win.", "<b>Build up.</b> Each chunk should use what the last one taught."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Keep it simple"}, {"html": "A good breakdown is simple, visual and easy to remember. Three clear steps are easier to follow than ten. For example, a greeting could be: <b>Step one: smile. Step two: introduce yourself. Step three: ask if it is their first time.</b> Three steps. Simple. Clear. Easy to remember. Easy to execute.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "If a step needs a long explanation, it is probably two steps.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Add what “good” looks like"}, {"items": ["For each step, say what correct looks like, such as a time, a temperature or a photo.", "Note the common mistakes, so you can watch for them."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 3, "title": "Sequence", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000328', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "These are examples to show the method. Your own SOPs always take priority. Replace any detail with your actual standards.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Front of house: greeting a new guest"}, {"html": "<b>Objective:</b> By the end of the session, the trainee will be able to greet every guest, introduce themselves and ask if it is their first visit.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Smile</b> and make eye contact. Standard: within the agreed time of entry.", "<b>Introduce yourself.</b> Say your name and welcome them.", "<b>Ask if it is their first visit.</b> If yes, briefly explain how we work."], "t": "ol"}, {"items": ["<b>Knowledge:</b> the agreed greeting time, how the restaurant works.", "<b>Skill:</b> clear, calm speech and an open posture.", "<b>Attitude:</b> warmth and genuine interest.", "<b>Common mistake:</b> greeting while looking at the screen."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Kitchen: changing the cutting board between tasks"}, {"html": "<b>Objective:</b> By the end of the session, the trainee will be able to select and use the correct colour-coded board for each food type and clean it between uses.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["<b>Choose the board.</b> Match the colour to the food type.", "<b>Prepare.</b> Check the board is clean and dry before use.", "<b>Use.</b> Keep different food types separate.", "<b>Clean.</b> Wash and sanitise before the next food type.", "<b>Store.</b> Return to the correct place."], "t": "ol"}, {"items": ["<b>Knowledge:</b> the colour code, why cross-contamination matters.", "<b>Skill:</b> washing and sanitising correctly.", "<b>Attitude:</b> not skipping a step because it is quiet.", "<b>Common mistake:</b> reusing a board for a different food type."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What to notice"}, {"items": ["Each example has one objective and a small number of steps.", "Every step starts with a verb.", "Knowledge, skill and attitude are all named.", "Common mistakes are listed, so the trainer knows what to watch for."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 4, "title": "Worked Examples: Front of House and Kitchen", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790842000000335', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "Instructions"}, {"html": "Choose one task from your own area that you will train in the next few weeks. Complete the template below. Share it with your manager for feedback.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Template"}, {"items": ["<b>Task name:</b>", "<b>Objective:</b> By the end of the session, the trainee will be able to…", "<b>Steps (3 to 7, each starting with a verb):</b>", "<b>For each step, what “good” looks like:</b>", "<b>Knowledge needed:</b>", "<b>Skill needed:</b>", "<b>Attitude or behaviour to see:</b>", "<b>Common mistakes to watch for:</b>", "<b>How you will check the trainee has learned it:</b>"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Check your work"}, {"items": ["Could a new hire follow it without you?", "Does it match the SOP?", "Are safety and hygiene steps included and early?", "Is every step observable?"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Submit through your manager, or discuss it in the next workshop. Do not use the breakdown for training until your manager has checked it matches the SOP.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790754626381655, "content": "", "minutes": 15, "pos": 5, "title": "Task: Break Down Your Own Task", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000007', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Most practice happens during real service. This is where trainees build speed and confidence, and where you can coach in the moment. It must be done carefully: the guest always comes first.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Rules for coaching during service"}, {"items": ["<b>Guest first.</b> Never coach in front of a guest. Step away, or wait.", "<b>Safety straight away.</b> If there is a safety or hygiene risk, step in immediately and calmly.", "<b>Save the detail.</b> Note other points and cover them after the rush."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Quick coaching techniques"}, {"items": ["<b>The quiet prompt.</b> “Remember the allergen question.”", "<b>The quick question.</b> “What comes next?” instead of telling them.", "<b>The thumbs-up.</b> A nod or quick “Good” when they get it right.", "<b>Shadow and swap.</b> You do one table, they do the next, while you watch."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "After the rush"}, {"items": ["Ask what went well and what was hard.", "Share one or two observations, with specific examples.", "Agree one thing to focus on next shift."], "t": "ol"}], "chapterId": 1790754638147226, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 3, "title": "Coaching on the Floor During Service", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000014', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Feedback is information about how someone is doing compared with the standard. Without it, a trainee does not know whether they are getting it right. Most people want to know how they are doing, even when they do not ask.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Two types of feedback"}, {"items": ["<b>Reinforcing feedback</b> tells someone what to keep doing. It builds confidence and repeats good behaviour.", "<b>Developing feedback</b> tells someone what to change. It closes the gap to the standard."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Trainees need both. A trainer who only corrects destroys confidence. A trainer who only praises lets standards slip.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Good feedback is…"}, {"items": ["<b>Timely:</b> soon after the event, while it is fresh.", "<b>Specific:</b> about one clear action, not a general impression.", "<b>About behaviour:</b> what they did, not who they are.", "<b>Private:</b> developing feedback is never given in front of guests or the team.", "<b>Linked to the standard:</b> “Our standard is…”"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "“Good job” and “That was wrong” are not feedback. They are verdicts. Feedback tells someone exactly what to repeat or change.", "t": "quote"}], "chapterId": 1790754665680843, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "Why Feedback Matters", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000021', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A simple structure keeps feedback clear, fair and short. Use the same structure for praise and for correction.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Situation, behaviour, impact, next step"}, {"items": ["<b>Situation:</b> when and where. “On table 6 just now…”", "<b>Behaviour:</b> exactly what they did, as you saw it. “…you repeated the order back to the guest.”", "<b>Impact:</b> the effect on the guest, team or business. “…so the guest felt confident it was right, and the kitchen got an accurate ticket.”", "<b>Next step:</b> what to keep doing or do differently. “Keep doing that on every table.”"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Example: developing feedback"}, {"html": "“During lunch on table 4 (<b>situation</b>), the drinks went out before the allergen question was asked (<b>behaviour</b>). If the guest had an allergy, the kitchen would not have known (<b>impact</b>). From now on, ask it before anything else is ordered. Let us practise the wording (<b>next step</b>).”", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Make it two-way"}, {"items": ["Ask first: “How do you think that went?” Many trainees will spot the issue themselves.", "Listen to their reason. There may be a problem you did not know about.", "Agree the next step together, and check they understood it.", "Follow up next shift, and recognise the improvement."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Avoid"}, {"items": ["The “compliment sandwich” every time. People learn to ignore the praise.", "Words like “always” and “never”.", "Feedback about personality: “You are careless.”", "Saving everything for a weekly review."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Use situation, behaviour, impact, next step.", "Ask for their view first, then give yours.", "Follow up and recognise improvement."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754665680843, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Giving Feedback That Works", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000028', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Praise is not just being nice. Specific praise tells a trainee exactly what good looks like, so they repeat it. It also builds the confidence they need to keep learning.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Make praise specific"}, {"items": ["<b>Weak:</b> “Well done today.”", "<b>Strong:</b> “You checked the temperature and recorded it without being reminded. That is exactly what we need.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "When to praise"}, {"items": ["When they meet a standard for the first time.", "When they fix something you gave feedback on.", "When they show the right attitude, such as helping a colleague or going the extra step for a guest.", "When they make good progress, even if not yet at standard."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Public or private?"}, {"html": "Praise in public can be powerful, but some people find it uncomfortable. If unsure, praise privately first. Corrections are always private.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Catch people doing things right. It is the fastest way to make the standard normal.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Be specific: name the action and why it was right.", "Praise progress as well as results.", "Correct in private. Praise in private or in public."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754665680843, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 2, "title": "Praise That Builds Confidence", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000035', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Sometimes a trainee repeats the same mistake, ignores a standard, is regularly late, or has an attitude that affects guests or the team. These conversations feel uncomfortable, so trainers often avoid them. Avoiding them makes the problem bigger.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Before the conversation"}, {"items": ["Get the facts: what happened, when, and how often. Avoid rumours.", "Check the standard or SOP so you can refer to it.", "Choose a private place and a calm time, not mid-service.", "Decide what outcome you want."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "During the conversation"}, {"items": ["<b>State the purpose.</b> “I want to talk about the allergen checks. It has happened three times this week.”", "<b>Describe the facts.</b> Use the situation, behaviour, impact structure.", "<b>Listen.</b> Ask: “Help me understand what is happening.” There may be a reason, such as a personal issue or unclear training.", "<b>Agree the expectation.</b> Restate the standard clearly.", "<b>Agree support and a next step.</b> Extra practice, a buddy, a check-in date.", "<b>Close and record.</b> Summarise what was agreed and note it in your records."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Stay calm and fair"}, {"items": ["Keep your voice level, even if they become upset or defensive.", "Talk about the behaviour, not the person.", "Do not make promises or threats about their job.", "If emotions run high, pause and continue later."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "When to involve your manager"}, {"items": ["The issue continues after you have discussed it and offered support.", "It involves safety, hygiene, harassment, discrimination or anything that may be a disciplinary matter.", "The trainee raises a personal or welfare concern.", "You feel out of your depth."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "As a trainer you address training and standards. Disciplinary action is for your manager. Escalate early, not late.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Prepare facts, the standard and a private place.", "Listen first, then agree the expectation and support.", "Escalate anything disciplinary, safety-related or personal to your manager."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754665680843, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 3, "title": "Difficult Conversations", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000042', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Good trainers keep improving. The people best placed to tell you how your training feels are the trainees and the managers who see the results.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Ask for it"}, {"items": ["At the end of a session: “What was most useful? What was unclear?”", "From your manager: “What do you see in the trainees I have trained?”", "From other trainers: compare methods and results."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Receive it well"}, {"items": ["Listen to understand, not to defend.", "Say thank you, even if you disagree.", "Ask for an example if it is vague.", "Decide on one thing to change, and try it."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Model the behaviour you want. If you take feedback well, your trainees will too.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Ask trainees, managers and peers for feedback.", "Listen, thank them, and act on one thing at a time."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754665680843, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 4, "title": "Receiving Feedback as a Trainer", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000049', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "When you sign off a lesson or a task, you are confirming that the trainee can do it to standard, on their own, consistently. It is not a record that the training happened. It is a record that the learning happened.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Sign-off means…"}, {"items": ["They can do it <b>to the standard</b> in the SOP.", "They can do it <b>without help</b> or reminders.", "They can do it <b>more than once</b>, including when it is busy.", "They <b>understand why</b> each key step matters."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Sign-off does not mean…"}, {"items": ["They sat through the session.", "They did it once with you beside them.", "They are trying hard.", "You ran out of time."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Why it matters"}, {"items": ["<b>Guests:</b> anyone signed off may serve them unsupervised.", "<b>Safety:</b> signing off food safety or allergen tasks too early creates real risk.", "<b>Fairness:</b> the trainee deserves an honest picture of where they are.", "<b>Trust:</b> managers rely on your sign-off to plan the rota."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754677963925, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 0, "title": "What Sign-off Means", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000056', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Observation is the main way to assess a skill. To be fair and consistent, observe against the written Key Performance Indicators, not against your personal way of doing things.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Use a checklist"}, {"html": "Turn the task breakdown from Module 3 into a checklist: one line per step, with what “good” looks like. Tick each step as you watch.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to observe"}, {"items": ["Tell the trainee you are observing, and what task.", "Let them work without interrupting, unless there is a safety risk.", "Watch the whole task, start to finish.", "Note facts: what they did and did not do.", "Observe more than once, at different times, including a busy period.", "Note down performance against any KPI&#39;s"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Avoid common errors"}, {"items": ["<b>First impressions:</b> judging the whole task by the first step.", "<b>Not Tracking KPI&#39;s: </b>having non-quantifiable measure of performance.", "<b>Halo effect:</b> assuming they are good at everything because they are good at one thing.", "<b>Personal style:</b> marking down a preference that still meets the standard.", "<b>Leniency:</b> passing someone because you like them, or to avoid an awkward talk."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Observe against a checklist from the task breakdown.", "Watch the whole task, more than once.", "Record facts, not impressions."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754677963925, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 1, "title": "Observing Against the Standard", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000063', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Observation shows what someone can do. Questions show whether they understand why, and what they would do when something unexpected happens.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Types of question"}, {"items": ["<b>Recall:</b> “What temperature must this be held at?”", "<b>Reason:</b> “Why do we ask about allergies before taking the order?”", "<b>Scenario:</b> “A guest says the dish is cold. What do you do?”", "<b>Show me:</b> “Show me where the spare stock is kept.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Poor questions to avoid"}, {"items": ["“Does that make sense?” (they will say yes)", "“You know what to do, right?”", "Questions with the answer built in: “You would check the label, wouldn’t you?”"], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Tips"}, {"items": ["Ask one question at a time and wait for the answer.", "Use real situations from your restaurant.", "If they get it wrong, do not just give the answer. Ask a follow-up to see where the gap is."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Combine observation with questions.", "Use reason and scenario questions, not just recall.", "Avoid questions that can be answered with a simple yes."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754677963925, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 2, "title": "Questioning to Check Understanding", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000070', $irr${"blocks": [{"items": ["Why finishing the course is not enough on its own: the relevant KPIs must be met too.", "How to tell a trainee they are not ready yet.", "What to do next."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Holding back a sign-off is part of the job. Handled well, it is motivating, because the trainee knows exactly what to work on.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Course complete is not the same as ready"}, {"html": "<b>You cannot sign off an employee if the relevant KPIs for their role are not being met, even if they have completed the course.</b> Finishing every lesson and passing the test shows they know the standard. The KPIs show whether they are delivering it on the floor, shift after shift.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Before you sign off, check the KPIs that apply to the trainee’s role, for example:", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Speed of service or ticket times", "Order accuracy and remakes", "Guest feedback and complaints", "Sales measures such as average spend or upselling", "Hygiene, safety and audit results"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "If you are not sure which KPIs apply to a role, or what the target is, ask your manager <i>before</i> the sign-off conversation, not after.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "Course complete + KPIs not met = <b>not signed off yet</b>.<br>Course complete + KPIs met + working to standard on their own = <b>ready to sign off</b>.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to say it"}, {"items": ["Start with what they do well, specifically.", "Name the gap clearly: “The step that is not yet at standard is…”, or “The KPI that is not yet on target is…”.", "Show them the number: where they are now and where they need to be.", "Explain why it matters.", "Agree a plan: extra practice, a buddy shift, and a date to review the KPI again."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "Example: “You’ve completed the course and passed the test, well done. Your greeting and order-taking are spot on. The one thing holding back your sign-off is order accuracy: you are at 92% and the target is 98%. Let’s focus on repeating the order back this week, and we’ll review your numbers together on Friday.”", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Escalate when…"}, {"items": ["They are still not at standard, or the KPIs are still off target, after the agreed extra support.", "The gap is a safety, hygiene or allergen issue.", "You think the training plan needs to change, or the person may not be suited to the role. That decision is your manager’s."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Completing the course does not earn a sign-off on its own. The relevant KPIs must be met.", "Be specific about the gap, show the number, and explain why it matters.", "Agree a plan and a date to review.", "Escalate repeated, KPI-related or safety-related gaps."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754677963925, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 3, "title": "When Not to Sign Off", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000077', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "If it is not recorded, it did not happen. iTrain is the record of who has been trained, by whom, and when.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Signing off a lesson"}, {"items": ["Open the course from your home page or dashboard.", "Go to the learner’s page.", "Tick each lesson they have completed to standard. iTrain records that you signed it off and when.", "Untick it if it was ticked by mistake."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Assigning the course test"}, {"items": ["When all lessons are signed off, assign the course test from the learner’s page.", "The test then appears on their home page.", "The course is complete only when they pass the test.", "Later, you can assign a retest to check what they remember."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Good habits"}, {"items": ["Sign off on the same day, after the session.", "Sign off only after a pass on the test assigned.", "Never sign off on someone else’s behalf.", "Check the dashboard weekly for learners who are overdue."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Sign off lessons in iTrain on the same day.", "Assign the course test when all lessons are signed off.", "Use retests to check what has been remembered."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754677963925, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 4, "title": "Recording Sign-off in iTrain", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000084', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A trainee who knows the plan feels supported. A plan also stops important training being forgotten when the restaurant is busy.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What a training plan includes"}, {"items": ["The courses and modules assigned in iTrain.", "Which tasks will be covered each week, in order.", "Who trains each part, and when.", "Deadlines for each chapter and for the course.", "When observations and the course test will happen."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Order of priority"}, {"items": ["<b>Objective</b>", "<b>Key Performance Indicators</b>", "<b>Time Taken for The Course</b>", "<b>Knowledge Required</b>", "<b>Skill Required</b>"], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Share the plan"}, {"items": ["Walk the trainee through it on day one.", "Agree check-in points, for example at the end of each week.", "Update it if they move faster or slower than expected."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "<i>Note:</i> the length of a training plan depends on the role and the restaurant. Agree the standard plan for each role with your manager.", "t": "p"}], "chapterId": 1790754697380164, "content": "", "minutes": 8, "pos": 0, "title": "Planning a Trainee's First Weeks", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000091', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "The most common reason training is not done is “we were too busy”. Training has to be planned into the week, like any other part of running the restaurant.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Find the right time"}, {"items": ["Use quieter periods: before opening, after lunch, early in the week.", "Avoid peak service for new skills. Use it for practice of skills already taught.", "Use the lesson times in iTrain to plan sessions that fit the time available."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Protect the time"}, {"items": ["Agree training time on the rota with your manager.", "Make sure the trainee is not also counted as full cover during the session.", "If a session has to move, reschedule it straight away. Do not just cancel."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Use small slots"}, {"items": ["A 10-minute pre-shift briefing on one menu item.", "A 15-minute practice after lunch service.", "A 30-minute knowledge or skill session (Module 5)."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Training that is not scheduled does not happen. Put it on the rota.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Plan training into quiet periods.", "Put it on the rota and protect it.", "Use short, regular sessions."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754697380164, "content": "", "minutes": 7, "pos": 1, "title": "Scheduling Training Around Service", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000098', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "A guest should get the same experience in each of our restaurants. Staff may also move between locations. That only works if every trainer teaches the same standards.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How to stay consistent"}, {"items": ["Teach from the SOP and the iTrain module, not from memory.", "Use the same task breakdowns and checklists.", "When a standard changes, check that every trainer has been told.", "Note genuine differences between restaurants, such as equipment or layout, and teach those clearly."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Hand-overs between trainers"}, {"items": ["Record what has been covered and signed off in iTrain.", "Note anything the next trainer should know, such as areas to focus on or concerns.", "Do not re-teach a task differently. If you think a standard is wrong, raise it with your manager."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Trainer catch-ups"}, {"html": "Meet other trainers regularly, even briefly. Share what is working, compare how you assess, and agree on any grey areas.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["One standard, taught the same way, everywhere.", "Record hand-overs in iTrain.", "Raise disagreements about a standard with your manager. Do not change it yourself."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754697380164, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 2, "title": "Consistency Across All Departments", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000105', $irr${"blocks": [{"html": "Records show what has been done and what is left. Follow-up makes sure the learning lasts after the session.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Keep these up to date"}, {"items": ["Lesson sign-offs in iTrain.", "Notes from observations and difficult conversations.", "Agreed action plans and reassessment dates.", "Concerns reported to your manager."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Follow up"}, {"items": ["Check a skill again a few shifts after sign-off.", "Ask a quick question at a pre-shift briefing.", "Use retests in iTrain to check what has been remembered.", "Recognise when someone is still doing it right."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Confidentiality"}, {"html": "Training records are about real people. Share them only with those who need them, such as the trainee and their manager. Follow your company’s policy on personal information.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Record sign-offs and notes on the same day.", "Follow up after sign-off to make sure it sticks.", "Keep trainee information confidential."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790754697380164, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 3, "title": "Keeping Records and Following Up", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000126', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "What you will learn"}, {"items": ["How to plan a fair post-trial observation.", "How to observe during live service without affecting the result.", "What to look for."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "A fair observation is planned, and the trainee knows it is happening. During the shift your job is to watch and record, not to train. If you step in, you are no longer seeing what they can do on their own.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Before the shift"}, {"items": ["Agree the date and shift with your manager. Choose a normal shift that includes a busy period.", "Prepare the observation checklist from the task breakdowns (Module 3).", "Only include tasks the trainee has been trained and signed off on.", "Tell the trainee in advance what will be observed and why."], "t": "ol"}, {"html": "<i>Example:</i> “On Friday’s dinner shift I will observe you working your section. I will look at greeting, order-taking, the allergen check and table clearing. Work as normal. I will only step in if there is a safety issue. We will talk it through afterwards.”", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "While observing"}, {"items": ["Position yourself where you can see without being in the way.", "Do not prompt or help, unless there is a safety, hygiene or allergen risk.", "Tick the checklist and write short factual notes with times.", "Watch both the quiet and the busy parts of the shift."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "What to look for"}, {"items": ["<b>Standards:</b> each step done to the SOP.", "<b>Consistency:</b> the same quality on every table or every ticket.", "<b>Under pressure:</b> what slips when it gets busy.", "<b>Guest interaction:</b> warmth, attention, handling problems.", "<b>Teamwork:</b> communication with colleagues and the kitchen."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Stay fair"}, {"items": ["Assess against the written standard, not your personal style.", "Watch out for first impressions and the halo effect (see Module 7).", "Judge the whole shift, not one moment."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "If you must step in"}, {"html": "Step in calmly, deal with the issue, and note it. A safety, hygiene or allergen lapse during the observation usually means the trainee is not ready yet.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Plan the shift and the checklist, and tell the trainee.", "Watch and record. Do not train during the observation.", "Step in only for safety, hygiene or allergens."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 2, "title": "Planning and Running the Observation", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000140', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "What you will learn"}, {"items": ["How to write observation notes that are fair and useful.", "How to debrief the trainee.", "The possible recommendations."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Your notes are the evidence for your recommendation. The debrief turns them into a clear message, so the trainee leaves knowing exactly where they stand.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Record facts, not opinions"}, {"items": ["<b>Opinion:</b> “Seemed disorganised.”", "<b>Fact:</b> “Two tables waited more than the agreed time for menus between 8:10 and 8:30.”", "<b>Opinion:</b> “Great with guests.”", "<b>Fact:</b> “Greeted returning guests by name and explained the specials without notes.”"], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Include what went well, any steps not at standard, anything that only happened when busy, and your recommendation with the reasons for it.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Debrief steps"}, {"html": "Debrief soon after the shift, in private.", "t": "p"}, {"items": ["Ask how they think it went.", "Share what went well, with examples.", "Share any gaps, with examples and why they matter.", "Explain your recommendation, and that the manager makes the final decision.", "Agree next steps."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Possible recommendations"}, {"items": ["<b>Ready:</b> works to standard independently. Released to the rota.", "<b>Ready with focus areas:</b> released, with specific points to follow up in a development plan.", "<b>Not ready yet:</b> more training needed on named tasks, with a development plan and a reassessment date."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Any recommendation about extending a trial, ending employment or a probation outcome goes to your manager. Do not discuss these outcomes with the trainee yourself unless your manager asks you to.", "t": "p"}, {"html": "No surprises. If the trainee has been getting honest feedback all along, the debrief should confirm what they already know.", "t": "quote"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Record facts with times and examples, balancing strengths and gaps.", "Debrief privately and soon.", "Base the recommendation on the evidence. Employment outcomes are your manager’s decision."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 10, "pos": 3, "title": "Recording, Debrief and Recommendation", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000154', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "What you will learn"}, {"items": ["How to turn an assessment or debrief into a plan."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "An assessment is only useful if it leads to action. Whether the trainee is “ready with focus areas” or “not ready yet”, a short development plan makes the next steps clear for them and for you.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "A simple plan"}, {"items": ["<b>Strengths:</b> what they do well.", "<b>Focus areas:</b> no more than two or three, each linked to a standard.", "<b>Actions:</b> what will be practised, how and with whom.", "<b>Dates:</b> when you will check again.", "<b>Support:</b> what you or the team will provide."], "t": "ol"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Example"}, {"items": ["<b>Focus area:</b> repeating orders back to guests.", "<b>Action:</b> practise on every table for the next three lunch shifts.", "<b>Support:</b> quick prompt from the section lead.", "<b>Check:</b> observation on Friday."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "Use chapter deadlines in iTrain to set dates for the plan, so the trainee and their manager can see them.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Name strengths and no more than three focus areas.", "Give each action a date and support.", "Use iTrain deadlines to track it."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 6, "pos": 4, "title": "Building a Development Plan", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb),
('lessons', '1790845000000161', $irr${"blocks": [{"t": "h3", "text": "What you will learn"}, {"items": ["Why knowledge fades.", "How to use retests and refreshers."], "t": "ul"}, {"html": "People forget what they do not use. A trainee who passed a test months ago may now be unsure, especially on rarely used procedures. Checking again keeps standards high.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "When to check again"}, {"items": ["A few weeks after the course test.", "When a menu, recipe or procedure changes.", "When you see a standard slipping.", "Before someone trains others."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "How"}, {"items": ["Assign a retest in iTrain from the learner’s page. Results are compared with their original score in Insights.", "Run a short refresher session on the topic.", "Ask quick questions at pre-shift briefings."], "t": "ul"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Mandatory training"}, {"html": "Some courses are mandatory and have a certificate that expires. iTrain shows who is compliant, expiring soon or expired. Check this regularly, and plan refreshers before certificates expire.", "t": "p"}, {"t": "h3", "text": "Key takeaways"}, {"items": ["Knowledge fades. Check it again.", "Use iTrain retests and short refreshers.", "Plan refreshers before mandatory certificates expire."], "t": "ul"}], "chapterId": 1790755313505141, "content": "", "minutes": 5, "pos": 5, "title": "Retests and Refreshers", "video": ""}$irr$::jsonb)
